WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.461 --> 00:00:06.300
Hi everybody.

00:00:06.301 --> 00:00:11.240
I'd like to welcome you
to our talk that's kind

00:00:11.241 --> 00:00:16.410
of an interesting hybrid of
programming languages and

00:00:16.411 --> 00:00:18.980
science, which usually,
I would say,

00:00:18.981 --> 00:00:22.190
the two often don't go together,
but should more often.

00:00:22.191 --> 00:00:24.560
So Andreas Stefik
is our speaker.

00:00:24.561 --> 00:00:27.280
He is from University of Nevada,
Las Vegas.

00:00:27.281 --> 00:00:32.260
And I first met him when he
was explaining statistics to

00:00:32.261 --> 00:00:34.870
a speaker of the conference,
that was better than

00:00:34.871 --> 00:00:38.180
the way that the speaker could
explain her own statistics.

00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:42.030
And that was pretty nice
cuz Andreas is really,

00:00:42.031 --> 00:00:46.390
he feels very strongly about
doing methodology properly.

00:00:46.391 --> 00:00:48.540
He is a very proponent of
randomized control trials

00:00:48.541 --> 00:00:50.690
especially when
you're dealing with

00:00:50.691 --> 00:00:53.310
religious type of questions
such as these things that

00:00:53.311 --> 00:00:56.020
will show up in programming
language design.

00:00:56.021 --> 00:00:58.640
Lots of questions have been
around since the beginning of

00:00:58.641 --> 00:01:01.930
programming languages that
are unsettled still to this day.

00:01:01.931 --> 00:01:05.105
Andreas has been doing a lot of
research into trying to figure

00:01:05.106 --> 00:01:07.782
out how to answer those
questions in a scientific,

00:01:07.783 --> 00:01:08.851
data-driven way.

00:01:08.852 --> 00:01:12.080
So let me hand it off to Andreas
to tell us all about that.

00:01:16.258 --> 00:01:17.777
&gt;&gt; All right, thanks for
inviting me, Andy.

00:01:17.778 --> 00:01:21.257
I appreciate it and I have the
distinct impression I'm going to

00:01:21.258 --> 00:01:23.040
enjoy the dinner later as well.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.920
There's a home cooked meal,
I've been promised.

00:01:26.921 --> 00:01:28.783
I'm gonna help, though.

00:01:28.784 --> 00:01:33.605
So as Andy said,
my name is Andy Stefik.

00:01:33.606 --> 00:01:36.986
I'm a computer scientist by
trade, but I've spent quite

00:01:36.987 --> 00:01:41.400
a lot of time with psychologists
and people sort of in that vein.

00:01:41.401 --> 00:01:43.680
And I've spent the last
few years, especially,

00:01:43.681 --> 00:01:47.500
sort of being especially
inspired by how other

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:51.330
fields solve really big
problems that the fields have.

00:01:51.331 --> 00:01:55.688
So I kinda wanna start with
this story that was actually

00:01:55.689 --> 00:02:00.243
a very famous test that
happened in the early 1800s.

00:02:00.244 --> 00:02:02.262
And so
let me sort of set the stage.

00:02:02.263 --> 00:02:04.943
There was this doctor,
it's 1834,

00:02:04.944 --> 00:02:07.960
his name's Friedrich Wilhelm
von Hoven.

00:02:07.961 --> 00:02:12.600
And at the time,
there were a variety of medical

00:02:12.601 --> 00:02:15.240
doctors and practitioners that
were advancing this technique

00:02:15.241 --> 00:02:19.760
that they called homeopathic
medicine, that was the name.

00:02:19.761 --> 00:02:23.390
It started in the late 1700s by
this guy named Samuel Haldeman

00:02:23.391 --> 00:02:27.510
and the idea was called
like cures like, right?

00:02:27.511 --> 00:02:31.540
It was that if you have
a condition that's bad, and

00:02:31.541 --> 00:02:33.230
you have a substance like that,

00:02:33.231 --> 00:02:35.320
then may be that will cure you,
right?

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:36.040
Is that a good idea?

00:02:36.041 --> 00:02:39.040
Well, it didn't hold
up to the evidence.

00:02:39.041 --> 00:02:42.120
However, the problem is that

00:02:42.121 --> 00:02:44.288
there was no way at that time
to really know for sure.

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:48.800
So von Hoven was really
skeptical that this might have

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:52.740
been fraud or at least he
wrote a letter about it.

00:02:52.741 --> 00:02:53.800
His letters are very famous,

00:02:53.801 --> 00:02:56.010
called The von Hoven Letter,
right?

00:02:56.011 --> 00:02:59.370
It went to a local newspaper
from a newspaper owner that

00:02:59.371 --> 00:03:02.550
was sort of anti-homeopathy,
if you will.

00:03:02.551 --> 00:03:05.760
And it's very hard to read,
it's in, at the time, in 1834,

00:03:05.761 --> 00:03:08.010
you can get the letter, but

00:03:08.011 --> 00:03:11.190
the German letters aren't
the letters that they are today.

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:14.567
So even though my colleagues and
I have expertise in German,

00:03:14.568 --> 00:03:17.610
it's very difficult to know
exactly what he's saying

00:03:17.611 --> 00:03:21.450
without someone expertise and
early 1800s Germanic languages.

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:22.970
But words like charlatan and

00:03:22.971 --> 00:03:25.390
fraud are used quite regularly
in this letter, right?

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:30.200
And what this led to was
actually a very famous test

00:03:30.201 --> 00:03:32.020
called the Nuremberg Salt Test.

00:03:32.021 --> 00:03:35.230
And it is widely regarded as
the first, what is called,

00:03:35.231 --> 00:03:36.270
randomized control trial.

00:03:36.271 --> 00:03:38.440
I'll get to this in a second.

00:03:38.441 --> 00:03:41.410
But here's the problem,
the problem was at the time,

00:03:41.411 --> 00:03:44.590
the doctors didn't really know
how to distinguish fact or

00:03:44.591 --> 00:03:47.470
belief from belief or
from fiction.

00:03:47.471 --> 00:03:50.300
They weren't sure how to
actually evaluate claims when

00:03:50.301 --> 00:03:52.589
someone made them about
a medical practice or

00:03:52.590 --> 00:03:53.379
other things.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.950
So for example, von Hoven,
when he put out his letter,

00:03:55.951 --> 00:03:58.168
he was challenged by
homeopathists of the day.

00:03:58.169 --> 00:04:01.048
Homeopathists that were
getting money to practice,

00:04:01.049 --> 00:04:02.971
especially from the aristocracy.

00:04:02.972 --> 00:04:04.353
They were getting paid to do it,
so

00:04:04.354 --> 00:04:06.166
they didn't want to
give up their practice,

00:04:06.167 --> 00:04:07.730
of course, right?

00:04:07.731 --> 00:04:09.254
And they didn't
trust each other.

00:04:09.255 --> 00:04:11.890
So von Hoven and his colleagues,
which were also doctors,

00:04:11.891 --> 00:04:15.240
they were saying things like,
well, we don't trust you, so

00:04:15.241 --> 00:04:17.600
if we give a treatment
to patients,

00:04:17.601 --> 00:04:19.800
we don't necessarily
trust them either.

00:04:19.801 --> 00:04:22.284
So we don't want them to know
what they've been treated with.

00:04:22.285 --> 00:04:23.860
And the homeopathists,
though, they're like, well,

00:04:23.861 --> 00:04:24.860
we don't trust you either.

00:04:24.861 --> 00:04:28.260
You're just gonna cheat and have
this fraudulent procedure, and

00:04:28.261 --> 00:04:30.290
then your methodology
will show that we're bad,

00:04:30.291 --> 00:04:33.570
even though we're not,
we're the good guys here.

00:04:33.571 --> 00:04:35.520
And at the same time,

00:04:35.521 --> 00:04:37.270
since neither one of
them trusted each other,

00:04:37.271 --> 00:04:40.400
they crafted these procedures
to sort of prevent fraud.

00:04:40.401 --> 00:04:43.555
And ironically, even though they
had fraud prevention procedures

00:04:43.556 --> 00:04:45.663
in the test,
people still committed fraud.

00:04:45.664 --> 00:04:49.280
And they were able to detect
it using some sly techniques.

00:04:49.281 --> 00:04:50.520
It was the early 1800s, right?

00:04:50.521 --> 00:04:52.600
So they weren't very good tests,
but

00:04:52.601 --> 00:04:56.780
they were orders of magnitude
better than what existed before.

00:04:56.781 --> 00:04:59.358
And because they didn't
know whether homeopathy had

00:04:59.359 --> 00:05:02.800
an impact, they also had
a non-homeopathic treatment.

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:04.648
And there's all sorts of
test around the period,

00:05:04.649 --> 00:05:05.950
some of them used things
like bread pills,

00:05:05.951 --> 00:05:08.370
which is bread in a pill,
or they used water,

00:05:08.371 --> 00:05:09.100
stuff like that.

00:05:09.101 --> 00:05:09.660
I know, shocking.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.010
But anyway,
as you can probably imagine,

00:05:13.011 --> 00:05:16.340
the theory of like cures like
didn't really stand up to

00:05:16.341 --> 00:05:18.670
the first experiment
a couple 100 years ago.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:22.560
And why do they care about that?

00:05:22.561 --> 00:05:23.800
There's really good reasons.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:27.290
Medical doctors of
the age wanted more data.

00:05:27.291 --> 00:05:31.180
He's hardly the only person of
the age that was very concerned,

00:05:31.181 --> 00:05:34.200
there was doctors that were
concerned about washing your

00:05:34.201 --> 00:05:36.250
hands cuz they were showing it
was killing pregnant women.

00:05:36.251 --> 00:05:38.290
There was all sorts of horrible,

00:05:38.291 --> 00:05:40.580
horrible things that actually
happen during the period,

00:05:40.581 --> 00:05:44.730
in part, because there wasn't
a lot of science going on

00:05:44.731 --> 00:05:48.780
in terms of our medical
practices of that age.

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:51.970
And of course, this led to
things like human death and

00:05:51.971 --> 00:05:57.950
suffering and money going
to the wrong treatments and

00:05:57.951 --> 00:06:01.310
fraud being sort of
propagated as a methodology,

00:06:01.311 --> 00:06:03.790
even though it didn't work.

00:06:03.791 --> 00:06:06.110
Or at least belief in something
incorrect, if not fraud.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:10.120
So interestingly,
although this was 1834,

00:06:10.121 --> 00:06:13.298
medical practices today still
have plenty of problems,

00:06:13.299 --> 00:06:15.495
as I imagine many of
you probably know.

00:06:15.496 --> 00:06:19.112
But there are procedures
now that are wildly,

00:06:19.113 --> 00:06:23.934
wildly beyond anything we see
in a field like computer science

00:06:23.935 --> 00:06:25.337
to detect fraud.

00:06:25.338 --> 00:06:28.314
Especially today, corporate
fraud, cuz this has been a major

00:06:28.315 --> 00:06:31.440
issue for a lot of reasons I
won't have time to go into.

00:06:31.441 --> 00:06:33.650
But there is registration
of experiments.

00:06:33.651 --> 00:06:36.520
There's replication
standards some fields have

00:06:36.521 --> 00:06:38.500
that required by
law in some cases.

00:06:38.501 --> 00:06:41.440
So it's very complicated and
tricky, but fascinating.

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:47.620
In any case, well,
I am starting with homeopathy.

00:06:47.621 --> 00:06:49.070
The reason for that is that
even though this talks

00:06:49.071 --> 00:06:52.430
about programming languages,
it turns out that there are some

00:06:52.431 --> 00:06:56.530
parallels between
the evidentiary practices

00:06:56.531 --> 00:07:00.640
of the early 1800s in medicine
to the evidentiary practices of

00:07:00.641 --> 00:07:03.490
computer science today,
with notable exceptions.

00:07:04.650 --> 00:07:05.660
And we know that this matters,

00:07:05.661 --> 00:07:07.340
programming languages
are sort of foundational,

00:07:07.341 --> 00:07:08.570
they're not the only
thing that matters.

00:07:08.571 --> 00:07:10.690
There's all sorts of
conditions that are important,

00:07:10.691 --> 00:07:14.720
like your processes, those
can matter, the technologies

00:07:14.721 --> 00:07:16.970
you're using, the IDEs that
you're using can matter.

00:07:16.971 --> 00:07:18.450
There's all sorts of factors and

00:07:18.451 --> 00:07:21.888
it's a very complicated space,
just like it is in other fields.

00:07:21.889 --> 00:07:23.650
It's not like
medicine is simple,

00:07:23.651 --> 00:07:26.975
right, it's an extraordinarily
complicated search

00:07:26.976 --> 00:07:28.290
base to try to navigate through.

00:07:29.404 --> 00:07:32.420
And these languages that we
have, even though it's only one

00:07:32.421 --> 00:07:35.390
part of the problem, it is an
important part of the problem,

00:07:35.391 --> 00:07:36.735
for reasons I'm going to get to.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:41.800
So for example, one thing that
you might think about right off

00:07:41.801 --> 00:07:45.620
is, how much actually money do
we pay software developers?

00:07:45.621 --> 00:07:48.150
So it's kind of a hard
question to get at,

00:07:48.151 --> 00:07:50.290
there isn't data
from every country.

00:07:50.291 --> 00:07:53.155
But if you just look at the US,
and you look at Bureau of

00:07:53.156 --> 00:07:56.600
Labor Statistics data, and then
you look at software engineers

00:07:56.601 --> 00:07:58.672
and people that sort
of support them.

00:07:58.673 --> 00:08:01.348
So you can't really know how
many of the use languages and

00:08:01.349 --> 00:08:04.243
how many don't, but you can kind
of get a sense of how much it is

00:08:04.244 --> 00:08:06.900
and we peg that from VLS,
data about $330 billion.

00:08:06.901 --> 00:08:10.339
All right, so it's not like
it's a small amount of money.

00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:12.449
It's very expensive to pay
software engineers and

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:13.240
that makes sense.

00:08:13.241 --> 00:08:15.104
Software engineers are highly
professional people.

00:08:15.105 --> 00:08:17.511
They do hard jobs,
they work long hours.

00:08:17.512 --> 00:08:19.274
It's a tricky thing,
it's important.

00:08:19.275 --> 00:08:22.550
And what that means though
is that given the size of

00:08:22.551 --> 00:08:23.564
the industry,

00:08:23.565 --> 00:08:27.152
even a small difference in
programming languages, or

00:08:27.153 --> 00:08:31.130
productivity by any metric,
can have a big impact at scale,

00:08:31.131 --> 00:08:35.810
even if you only consider our
country, not necessarily others.

00:08:35.811 --> 00:08:39.800
So, let's look at a case study
from Google on compiler errors,

00:08:39.801 --> 00:08:41.430
just to get a sense.

00:08:41.431 --> 00:08:43.678
So as you know,
Google tracks everything,

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:46.777
and one of those things in the
subset of everything happens to

00:08:46.778 --> 00:08:48.871
be compiler errors
of their employees.

00:08:48.872 --> 00:08:52.670
And if you look at that, they
published a paper a few years

00:08:52.671 --> 00:08:56.789
ago on having tracked about
26.5 million compiler errors

00:08:56.790 --> 00:09:00.380
from software engineers
using either C++ or Java.

00:09:00.381 --> 00:09:03.070
Now, if you look at the data,
and we cut this up into

00:09:03.071 --> 00:09:05.600
various ways and make a certain
assumptions that I can get into

00:09:05.601 --> 00:09:09.086
later if you want, but it looks
at, Java developers had about

00:09:09.087 --> 00:09:13.346
29.7% of the time,
they had compile errors, and for

00:09:13.347 --> 00:09:19.260
C++ it was a little
higher 37.4%.

00:09:19.261 --> 00:09:21.710
Again, these are experienced
software engineers

00:09:21.711 --> 00:09:23.978
working in the industry
at a major tech company.

00:09:23.979 --> 00:09:25.137
Yeah.

00:09:25.138 --> 00:09:28.960
&gt;&gt; What type of errors
are [INAUDIBLE]?

00:09:28.961 --> 00:09:31.390
&gt;&gt; These are errors where
the compiler rejected it, and so

00:09:31.391 --> 00:09:32.950
they come in a variety
of categories, and

00:09:32.951 --> 00:09:34.660
they tried to categorize them.

00:09:34.661 --> 00:09:36.800
If you look on
the right on the graph,

00:09:36.801 --> 00:09:39.530
these are the kinds of
categories that they gave.

00:09:39.531 --> 00:09:42.170
But as you can probably imagine
sometimes it's a little

00:09:42.171 --> 00:09:42.900
bit fuzzy,

00:09:42.901 --> 00:09:46.280
if the compiler doesn't know
exactly what the problem was.

00:09:46.281 --> 00:09:48.736
But other is a pretty
small category and

00:09:48.737 --> 00:09:51.938
most of the time it was
things like dependencies and

00:09:51.939 --> 00:09:54.465
type mismatch and
stuff like that. Yeah.

00:09:54.466 --> 00:09:55.385
&gt;&gt; So, is it,

00:09:55.386 --> 00:09:59.157
I see this on the bottom
you say percent of an eight

00:09:59.158 --> 00:10:01.266
hour work day,-
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:10:01.267 --> 00:10:07.223
&gt;&gt; But it looks like
the percentages sunk to 100.

00:10:07.224 --> 00:10:08.582
&gt;&gt; Okay.
Okay, okay.

00:10:08.583 --> 00:10:11.658
&gt;&gt; Developers certainly don't
spend all of their eight hour

00:10:11.659 --> 00:10:13.940
work day fixing compiler issues,
right?

00:10:13.941 --> 00:10:15.713
&gt;&gt; No, no,
no that's the point, in fact,

00:10:15.714 --> 00:10:19.010
it comes out to be like 20-25
minutes or something of a day.

00:10:19.011 --> 00:10:22.850
So, the point isn't so much that
that's not 100% of their day,

00:10:22.851 --> 00:10:25.070
that's 100% of time, right?

00:10:25.071 --> 00:10:28.775
I think is what their
graph means, so.

00:10:28.776 --> 00:10:31.761
So if we made an assumption that
this was an eight hour work,

00:10:31.762 --> 00:10:33.613
which is probably not fair,
right,

00:10:33.614 --> 00:10:36.092
cuz people are gonna vary,
and stuff like that.

00:10:36.093 --> 00:10:39.950
But if we made that assumption
for the sake of argument, and

00:10:39.951 --> 00:10:43.577
we assume that Google's data
is accurate and honest and

00:10:43.578 --> 00:10:47.512
all that kind of stuff as well,
then this comes out to be about

00:10:47.513 --> 00:10:51.939
$5.9 billion for Java at scale,
and about 4.48 for C++.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:53.283
Those are lots of
assumptions there.

00:10:53.284 --> 00:10:56.577
We can't say that that happens
at scale, cuz we don't know.

00:10:56.578 --> 00:11:00.147
But nonetheless, at least
at Google, it's enough of

00:11:00.148 --> 00:11:03.367
money during the day that it's
a nontrivial amount of cash.

00:11:03.368 --> 00:11:04.270
Yeah.

00:11:04.271 --> 00:11:07.214
&gt;&gt; Actually it is just an
argument I would think, because

00:11:07.215 --> 00:11:10.542
they haven't said anything,
or they didn't say anything,

00:11:10.543 --> 00:11:13.745
about what actually is gonna
happen for that 25 minutes.

00:11:13.746 --> 00:11:16.167
Maybe the programmers just
go home earlier everyday.

00:11:16.168 --> 00:11:18.885
It doesn't necessarily
translate into actual money or

00:11:18.886 --> 00:11:19.703
productivity.

00:11:19.704 --> 00:11:21.911
&gt;&gt; I would agree with that,
yeah, we don't know that for

00:11:21.912 --> 00:11:22.874
sure, you're right.

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:25.741
&gt;&gt; It says percentage
of build errors,

00:11:25.742 --> 00:11:27.761
it doesn't say
anything about time.

00:11:27.762 --> 00:11:32.081
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry, yes,
I apologise, yes this is, okay.

00:11:32.082 --> 00:11:33.779
So they broke into
two categories,

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:36.780
one of the categories was time,
and I gave you those numbers for

00:11:36.781 --> 00:11:39.496
just that piece, and then this
is the percentage of build

00:11:39.497 --> 00:11:42.465
errors in terms of like how many
were they in this categories.

00:11:42.466 --> 00:11:46.712
Thank you, you're right, that's
exactly what it's pointing out.

00:11:46.713 --> 00:11:50.165
Okay, in addition, as it
turns out, besides industry,

00:11:50.166 --> 00:11:53.406
education in the United States
at least in, not just in

00:11:53.407 --> 00:11:56.998
the United States, but a bunch
of countries, are investing

00:11:56.999 --> 00:12:00.040
quite a bit in computer
science for K through 12.

00:12:00.041 --> 00:12:02.815
This isn't just industry,
this isn't just government,

00:12:02.816 --> 00:12:04.094
it's a little bit of both.

00:12:04.095 --> 00:12:06.735
So if you look at expenditures
from this report called

00:12:06.736 --> 00:12:09.442
the BNY Mellon report it
doesn't look like that much.

00:12:09.443 --> 00:12:12.970
It's like $12 or $13 million
across the states over the last

00:12:12.971 --> 00:12:15.490
two years,
not exactly a lot of a cash.

00:12:15.491 --> 00:12:17.304
Even the amount that Microsoft,

00:12:17.305 --> 00:12:20.737
I don't remember the exact
numbers, has given to code.org,

00:12:20.738 --> 00:12:23.991
significantly dwarfs this
overall, for all 50 states.

00:12:23.992 --> 00:12:27.367
New York City gave $80 million
over a ten-year period, and

00:12:27.368 --> 00:12:31.123
the Obama administration, before
it left, pegged it, if I recall,

00:12:31.124 --> 00:12:34.201
around $4 billion is what is
necessary to really do it.

00:12:34.202 --> 00:12:35.665
Now, does that mean it
will really happen?

00:12:35.666 --> 00:12:38.958
I don't know, but
the point is that there's quite

00:12:38.959 --> 00:12:42.712
a few government and industry
groups interested in having

00:12:42.713 --> 00:12:46.388
computer science lower in part
because BLS data suggests

00:12:46.389 --> 00:12:49.467
that industry needs more
software engineers.

00:12:49.468 --> 00:12:52.169
It's a major problem that you
can look up very easily for

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:53.157
that type of stuff.

00:12:53.158 --> 00:12:57.338
However, the thing that's funny
is that in order to teach

00:12:57.339 --> 00:13:01.091
computer science at that
level you have to teach it in

00:13:01.092 --> 00:13:02.044
something.

00:13:02.045 --> 00:13:06.124
So it has to be in a programming
language unless you simply

00:13:06.125 --> 00:13:09.354
choose to do all hand
gestures or something or

00:13:09.355 --> 00:13:12.077
just conceptually
you could do that.

00:13:12.078 --> 00:13:14.308
And it turns out there's
two major initiatives,

00:13:14.309 --> 00:13:16.708
one of them is called,
exploring computer science.

00:13:16.709 --> 00:13:20.194
This is an approach that was put
out originally by Jane Margolis

00:13:20.195 --> 00:13:23.416
out of UCLA, and it's a very
specific curriculum that has

00:13:23.417 --> 00:13:26.836
made specific choices about what
you do in the classroom, and

00:13:26.837 --> 00:13:28.385
it happens to be polyglot.

00:13:28.386 --> 00:13:31.349
So it uses seven programming
languages, so long as you define

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:34.085
programming language a little
more broadly than true and

00:13:34.086 --> 00:13:34.657
complete.

00:13:34.658 --> 00:13:36.810
So if you count
HTML as a language,

00:13:36.811 --> 00:13:38.750
then that would be a language.

00:13:38.751 --> 00:13:41.397
If you don't,
then it would be a handful.

00:13:42.530 --> 00:13:44.350
The point is,
it's high school curriculum that

00:13:44.351 --> 00:13:45.720
uses seven programming
languages for

00:13:45.721 --> 00:13:46.930
whatever reason, and
I'm not defending or

00:13:46.931 --> 00:13:48.600
refuting the position,
I'm saying that's a fact.

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:52.910
And one research team in
the United States picks it for

00:13:52.911 --> 00:13:56.140
the whole United States,
that's how that works.

00:13:56.141 --> 00:14:00.240
The second option that some
schools are doing that's sort of

00:14:00.241 --> 00:14:03.800
up and coming options, is called
Computer Science Principles.

00:14:03.801 --> 00:14:05.200
And so, in this particular case,

00:14:05.201 --> 00:14:07.530
students basically take a test,
and if they do well,

00:14:07.531 --> 00:14:09.380
they can get a little bit
of college credit for it.

00:14:09.381 --> 00:14:12.441
No one knows what college
credit, but it exists.

00:14:12.442 --> 00:14:14.301
This is programming
language neutral,

00:14:14.302 --> 00:14:15.839
which has the opposite problem.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:19.741
Since there's no one choice for
language, or even seven choices,

00:14:19.742 --> 00:14:23.644
that means individual curriculum
designers make their own thing,

00:14:23.645 --> 00:14:26.724
and as such a student in
Alabama might have a different

00:14:26.725 --> 00:14:30.140
conception of what a loop is
than someone in Washington.

00:14:30.141 --> 00:14:32.887
Now again, I'm not saying
that's good or bad,

00:14:32.888 --> 00:14:34.702
I'm saying that that's true.

00:14:34.703 --> 00:14:36.509
And these standards
are very different,

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:38.630
they're ratified by
the college board.

00:14:38.631 --> 00:14:41.355
So they've been vetted by
a great number of computer

00:14:41.356 --> 00:14:43.020
scientists over
a many year period.

00:14:43.021 --> 00:14:46.196
And they're pretty sensible,
I mean like they say,

00:14:46.197 --> 00:14:48.316
you should understand iteration,

00:14:48.317 --> 00:14:51.363
you should understand
conditionals, whatever.

00:14:51.364 --> 00:14:52.965
And if you look in college,

00:14:52.966 --> 00:14:57.700
there's also no real consistent
sort of guidelines or standards.

00:14:57.701 --> 00:15:01.457
So a few years back, we did an
analysis of just 39 colleges in

00:15:01.458 --> 00:15:05.214
the Midwest and between them
they use C, C++, Alice, VB,

00:15:05.215 --> 00:15:07.474
Python, Java, C#, COBOL, ADA.

00:15:07.475 --> 00:15:09.148
And if you talk to
these individuals-

00:15:09.149 --> 00:15:10.643
&gt;&gt; Isn't COBOL [INAUDIBLE]?

00:15:10.644 --> 00:15:12.680
&gt;&gt; Actually there's-
&gt;&gt; Like what is

00:15:12.681 --> 00:15:15.060
the name of the university
that uses COBOL [LAUGH]?

00:15:15.061 --> 00:15:17.785
&gt;&gt; You'd be surprised, there's a
whole bunch of universities that

00:15:17.786 --> 00:15:19.741
use COBOL, and it's for
a really good reason.

00:15:19.742 --> 00:15:23.680
And one of them is because
during Y2K, they found that on

00:15:23.681 --> 00:15:28.113
average, if you took a class on
COBOL, during that period, you

00:15:28.114 --> 00:15:33.572
on average got $10,000 more on
hire than you would otherwise.

00:15:33.573 --> 00:15:36.550
So as a university, it made
a lot of sense in that period to

00:15:36.551 --> 00:15:38.360
do that, because of legacy code.

00:15:38.361 --> 00:15:40.919
So I completely agree,
it's insane,

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:43.533
but on the other hand-
&gt;&gt; The year 2000 only comes

00:15:43.534 --> 00:15:44.439
around once, right?

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.920
&gt;&gt; Well, we'll get to that, so-
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:46.921 --> 00:15:49.431
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH] I know.

00:15:49.432 --> 00:15:50.480
&gt;&gt; They still use COBOL.

00:15:50.481 --> 00:15:51.902
&gt;&gt; Yeah, but in any case,

00:15:51.903 --> 00:15:54.827
I'm absolutely not
defending the use of COBOL.

00:15:54.828 --> 00:15:57.515
I'm just saying that,
when you go talk to people that

00:15:57.516 --> 00:16:00.455
use particular languages,
they sort of fell into camps.

00:16:00.456 --> 00:16:02.538
One group of people would say,

00:16:02.539 --> 00:16:05.833
I use it because that's
what I'm told to use,.

00:16:05.834 --> 00:16:08.528
And then other people would say,

00:16:08.529 --> 00:16:12.964
I very strongly believe that
this is the right choice.

00:16:12.965 --> 00:16:16.133
The individual,
whoever it may be that used ADA,

00:16:16.134 --> 00:16:18.321
I think there's only one school,

00:16:18.322 --> 00:16:21.352
really believed in that
particular approach.

00:16:21.353 --> 00:16:24.203
Now, maybe he's right,
or she is right, but

00:16:24.204 --> 00:16:26.410
the data doesn't
seem clear to me.

00:16:28.680 --> 00:16:32.470
And as it happens,
these tools they are dispersed,

00:16:32.471 --> 00:16:35.581
they have dispersion of designs,
right?

00:16:35.582 --> 00:16:37.723
And it could be that
this doesn't matter or

00:16:37.724 --> 00:16:39.098
could be that does it does.

00:16:39.099 --> 00:16:42.077
So for example,
one language might say For

00:16:42.078 --> 00:16:44.573
left paren equals 0, 1.

00:16:44.574 --> 00:16:48.270
1 minus 10, 1 less than or
equal to 10, semicolon, i++,

00:16:48.271 --> 00:16:49.333
paren left brace.

00:16:49.334 --> 00:16:53.730
System.out.println, left paren,
double quote hello, minus,

00:16:53.731 --> 00:16:57.745
double quote plus i, right
paren, semicolon, right brace,

00:16:57.746 --> 00:16:59.550
rolls off the tongue.

00:16:59.551 --> 00:17:00.730
Another might say stuff like,

00:17:00.731 --> 00:17:04.090
repeat ten times,
output hello, stuff like that.

00:17:04.091 --> 00:17:05.915
I'm leaving stuff out,
but you get the idea.

00:17:05.916 --> 00:17:09.226
&gt;&gt; Even for
trivial traces, actually,

00:17:09.227 --> 00:17:13.264
it does shows dispersion for
trivial traces.

00:17:13.265 --> 00:17:16.885
But those languages actually
don't have a lot of dispersion

00:17:16.886 --> 00:17:20.364
where concept, it wasn't like
you were saying, list and

00:17:20.365 --> 00:17:22.068
then [INAUDIBLE] passed out.

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:25.163
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think that's actually
true, so a lot of languages,

00:17:25.164 --> 00:17:28.020
they don't show dispersion for
what their goals are, or

00:17:28.021 --> 00:17:29.890
what they're trying to achieve.

00:17:29.891 --> 00:17:33.207
They show dispersion in
the actual design to achieve it,

00:17:33.208 --> 00:17:36.741
yeah, that's a good point,
that's totally true, yeah.

00:17:36.742 --> 00:17:40.221
And here's a good example,
so generally speaking,

00:17:40.222 --> 00:17:44.730
most languages have this concept
of string concatenation, right?

00:17:44.731 --> 00:17:46.620
Being Microsoft,
I'm going to go on a limb and

00:17:46.621 --> 00:17:48.650
say I don't have to
explain that concept.

00:17:48.651 --> 00:17:50.740
And if you look at
the top example,

00:17:50.741 --> 00:17:53.705
which happens to be Java,
then this concatenates.

00:17:53.706 --> 00:17:55.840
If you look at
the example in the middle,

00:17:55.841 --> 00:17:57.090
it happens to not, right?

00:17:57.091 --> 00:17:59.826
Now, does it matter either way
what the choice is, maybe,

00:17:59.827 --> 00:18:00.386
maybe not.

00:18:00.387 --> 00:18:03.132
You could debate it, but
at the end of the day,

00:18:03.133 --> 00:18:04.476
there is dispersion.

00:18:04.477 --> 00:18:05.964
And in that context,
that means that

00:18:05.965 --> 00:18:08.130
the design leads to
a different conclusion, right?

00:18:08.131 --> 00:18:09.640
It doesn't do the same thing,

00:18:09.641 --> 00:18:11.120
despite the fact that
we could agree or

00:18:11.121 --> 00:18:13.940
disagree on whether the string
concatenation should be allowed

00:18:13.941 --> 00:18:14.660
on a plus.

00:18:14.661 --> 00:18:16.540
In other words,
as computer scientists,

00:18:16.541 --> 00:18:19.090
we don't have much agreement
on even very trivial things.

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:23.270
And you might think that
this doesn't matter, but

00:18:23.271 --> 00:18:25.700
in fact,
some types of technologies,

00:18:25.701 --> 00:18:27.740
the dispersion gets
a little wackier.

00:18:27.741 --> 00:18:30.480
So a good example is the web,
when the web was being

00:18:30.481 --> 00:18:36.070
developed, it started with
things like HTTP protocol and

00:18:36.071 --> 00:18:38.690
HTML, and
these sort of technologies.

00:18:38.691 --> 00:18:41.940
And over time, we had different
needs, we needed to run it on

00:18:41.941 --> 00:18:45.246
a server all of a sudden,
and have it take in logins.

00:18:45.247 --> 00:18:47.546
And we wanted to manage
formatting in an easier way, so

00:18:47.547 --> 00:18:48.548
we started making CSS.

00:18:48.549 --> 00:18:51.345
And then someone was like,
I don't like CSS,

00:18:51.346 --> 00:18:54.890
I need LESS, or SASS, or
these other technologies.

00:18:54.891 --> 00:18:55.490
And they're different,

00:18:55.491 --> 00:18:58.730
they're not compatible
necessarily, right?

00:18:58.731 --> 00:19:00.100
TypeScript, Dart,
I mean, you name it,

00:19:00.101 --> 00:19:02.720
right, on the server side,
it's the same situation.

00:19:02.721 --> 00:19:04.900
There's a great deal
of technologies, and

00:19:04.901 --> 00:19:08.720
they do very similar or
the same things.

00:19:08.721 --> 00:19:10.100
But they do it in
a different way,

00:19:10.101 --> 00:19:12.870
there's dispersion,
that's what I mean by that.

00:19:12.871 --> 00:19:13.920
And the same with the database,

00:19:13.921 --> 00:19:16.890
there's many variants of SQL,
NoSQL, stuff like that.

00:19:16.891 --> 00:19:19.334
Again, this isn't good or
bad necessarily,

00:19:19.335 --> 00:19:21.096
without evidence to support it.

00:19:21.097 --> 00:19:24.953
It's just true, right, it's
that this dispersion exists,

00:19:24.954 --> 00:19:28.082
right, and this has side
effects, potentially,

00:19:28.083 --> 00:19:30.000
it may work in different ways.

00:19:30.001 --> 00:19:31.915
So let's take this Y2K example,

00:19:31.916 --> 00:19:35.500
which I happened to have
prepared ahead of time.

00:19:35.501 --> 00:19:41.010
And as it turns out, during the
late 1990s, it's very difficult

00:19:41.011 --> 00:19:43.492
to get good data on how
much it cost to fix Y2K,

00:19:43.493 --> 00:19:45.690
and we've looked to the lot
of sources on this.

00:19:47.710 --> 00:19:50.740
The best estimates we've seen so
far seem to peg it

00:19:50.741 --> 00:19:55.460
between 300 and
800 billion for United States.

00:19:55.461 --> 00:19:58.567
But we don't know for
sure how much it cost, and

00:19:58.568 --> 00:20:01.839
if nothing else,
it seems like it's expensive.

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:03.940
Some countries track
it better than others,

00:20:03.941 --> 00:20:06.760
some countries have their own
estimates, and they have some

00:20:06.761 --> 00:20:10.150
evidence to support them, but
it's tricky to find out exactly.

00:20:10.151 --> 00:20:10.820
But nonetheless,

00:20:10.821 --> 00:20:13.800
it appears to have been
an expensive fix, right?

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:19.040
And Y2K only comes around once,
but the problem is,

00:20:19.041 --> 00:20:21.740
as we progress as a discipline
over the next 40 or

00:20:21.741 --> 00:20:25.130
50 years, it's not like
maintenance goes away.

00:20:25.131 --> 00:20:30.350
So today, you might write
your web page with PHP and

00:20:30.351 --> 00:20:33.830
HTML, and CSS, and SQL,
and whatever, right?

00:20:33.831 --> 00:20:35.240
But in 40 years,

00:20:35.241 --> 00:20:37.760
it could be that someone has
to maintain some of that code.

00:20:37.761 --> 00:20:40.770
Some of it will die, and some of
it will need to be maintained.

00:20:40.771 --> 00:20:43.360
So the question is, which
languages will live and die,

00:20:43.361 --> 00:20:45.332
what is the evidence for
or against them?

00:20:45.333 --> 00:20:47.627
And what's the cost of
maintaining a site built with N

00:20:47.628 --> 00:20:48.749
programming languages?

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:52.280
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Chaucer,
stuff like that,

00:20:52.281 --> 00:20:55.940
you have the same
kinds of problems.

00:20:55.941 --> 00:20:59.455
You have to learn a whole bunch
of new stuff to read Chaucer

00:20:59.456 --> 00:21:00.480
[INAUDIBLE].

00:21:00.481 --> 00:21:03.310
&gt;&gt; That's right, so the question
is, what does that cost, right,

00:21:03.311 --> 00:21:05.895
that's the question,
longitudinally.

00:21:05.896 --> 00:21:08.267
&gt;&gt; You'd end up with
[INAUDIBLE] stack,

00:21:08.268 --> 00:21:12.560
and the fact that there's actual
benefits from things changing.

00:21:12.561 --> 00:21:15.461
In terms of adaptivity of
language requirements, and

00:21:15.462 --> 00:21:17.232
what people are trying to solve.

00:21:17.233 --> 00:21:20.160
I'm just curious if you
factor the benefit and

00:21:20.161 --> 00:21:22.130
not just the cost.

00:21:22.131 --> 00:21:23.750
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I don't think we disagree,

00:21:23.751 --> 00:21:25.478
I think that this
is a cost-benefit,

00:21:25.479 --> 00:21:26.614
that's exactly right.

00:21:26.615 --> 00:21:29.340
But my point is that we don't
have a good estimate of what

00:21:29.341 --> 00:21:32.357
those are, right, so I don't
think we disagree at all, so.

00:21:32.358 --> 00:21:36.356
&gt;&gt; Do you think that this sort
of [INAUDIBLE] web maintenance

00:21:36.357 --> 00:21:39.335
where things are change
in a very fluid and

00:21:39.336 --> 00:21:43.349
constant fashion has
an impact on the issue of?

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:47.384
&gt;&gt; I don't know, it sounds
plausible, but I'm not sure,

00:21:47.385 --> 00:21:49.487
I don't know of any
direct evidence for or

00:21:49.488 --> 00:21:51.066
against the hypothesis, so.

00:21:51.067 --> 00:21:53.343
There's one last issue I wanna
mention with this version that

00:21:53.344 --> 00:21:55.137
I think people don't think
about that much, and

00:21:55.138 --> 00:21:57.150
that is accessibility
technologies.

00:21:57.151 --> 00:22:00.420
And when you look at
undergraduates in STEM,

00:22:00.421 --> 00:22:04.060
about 11% of students have
documented disabilities,

00:22:04.061 --> 00:22:06.730
it's a huge range, right?

00:22:06.731 --> 00:22:10.080
People on the autism spectrum,
blind people, deaf people,

00:22:10.081 --> 00:22:11.320
you name it, right?

00:22:11.321 --> 00:22:14.180
And as it happens,
some of these technologies have

00:22:14.181 --> 00:22:16.050
real problems because
of dispersion.

00:22:16.051 --> 00:22:19.170
And they're well known in
the accessibility community, so

00:22:19.171 --> 00:22:21.810
a good example is blind
accessible technologies.

00:22:21.811 --> 00:22:23.950
If you want to make your
technology blind accessible,

00:22:23.951 --> 00:22:25.985
and you're using specifically
Microsoft products,

00:22:25.986 --> 00:22:26.900
you're in good shape.

00:22:26.901 --> 00:22:29.660
Because you guys actually did
a great job, and that is,

00:22:29.661 --> 00:22:31.501
you made [INAUDIBLE]
accessible two.

00:22:31.502 --> 00:22:34.208
Which ended up being
a standard in certain ways,

00:22:34.209 --> 00:22:37.652
in other platforms, has all
sorts of good properties, etc.

00:22:37.653 --> 00:22:41.659
However, if you want your
application to work on phones

00:22:41.660 --> 00:22:44.047
and Mac, and all sorts of stuff,

00:22:44.048 --> 00:22:48.071
there are different technologies
you have to code for.

00:22:48.072 --> 00:22:48.870
They work differently,

00:22:48.871 --> 00:22:50.680
and sometimes they
don't work at all.

00:22:50.681 --> 00:22:54.350
So for example, if you have
Oracle's accessibility

00:22:54.351 --> 00:22:58.350
technology behind the scenes,
it is mostly incompatible for

00:22:58.351 --> 00:22:59.890
screen reading technologies.

00:22:59.891 --> 00:23:00.570
It's okay,

00:23:00.571 --> 00:23:04.120
it works sometimes, but
not very well, so it's tricky.

00:23:04.121 --> 00:23:06.650
The point is that this
version isn't just,

00:23:06.651 --> 00:23:08.500
my plus operator is different.

00:23:08.501 --> 00:23:12.080
It's that since there's both
a dispersion of the technologies

00:23:12.081 --> 00:23:14.230
and a dispersion of
the implementations.

00:23:14.231 --> 00:23:16.990
What you end up with
is inconsistency for

00:23:16.991 --> 00:23:20.492
different kinds of people
like the blind, right?

00:23:20.493 --> 00:23:23.624
That's the idea, but the problem
is, this is one of those nasty

00:23:23.625 --> 00:23:25.481
problems that we've
talked about for

00:23:25.482 --> 00:23:27.290
a long time in computer science.

00:23:27.291 --> 00:23:29.863
I'm not really telling you
something you don't know,

00:23:29.864 --> 00:23:30.580
necessarily.

00:23:30.581 --> 00:23:33.836
We all know we have different
programming languages that we

00:23:33.837 --> 00:23:36.477
have to pay developers money,
that's clear.

00:23:36.478 --> 00:23:39.950
And this is also one of those
problems where one individual

00:23:39.951 --> 00:23:43.370
scholar or industry
professional or even CEO

00:23:43.371 --> 00:23:46.760
probably can't fix it on their
own, it's probably not possible.

00:23:46.761 --> 00:23:50.419
So the question is, where there
other fields in science that had

00:23:50.420 --> 00:23:52.468
big problems, and
what do they do?

00:23:52.469 --> 00:23:54.988
To try to actually make
progress on problems that were

00:23:54.989 --> 00:23:57.676
bigger than themselves,
all right, how did we do that?

00:23:57.677 --> 00:24:01.552
And it turns out one of the most
common ways was, number one,

00:24:01.553 --> 00:24:03.224
getting more evidence and

00:24:03.225 --> 00:24:06.860
trying to hammer it down
very quantitatively.

00:24:06.861 --> 00:24:10.110
In addition, in some fields but
not others, there

00:24:10.111 --> 00:24:13.950
were also political movements,
so we'll talk about that a bit.

00:24:15.270 --> 00:24:19.247
So one interesting thing
written by a paper by you two,

00:24:19.248 --> 00:24:21.936
actually, you wrote this too?

00:24:21.937 --> 00:24:24.325
You're Devanbu, great,
I love this paper, so.

00:24:24.326 --> 00:24:26.593
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Well, it's true,

00:24:26.594 --> 00:24:28.370
I do really like this paper.

00:24:28.371 --> 00:24:31.352
So these three guys right
here happened to write this

00:24:31.353 --> 00:24:35.572
really fantastic paper out of,
I think it was right, but, yes.

00:24:35.573 --> 00:24:38.599
And they found something I
found really compelling,

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:42.161
I had no idea you guys would be
here, I didn't like plant this,

00:24:42.162 --> 00:24:45.117
I promise, but-
&gt;&gt; It's okay if you did.

00:24:45.118 --> 00:24:47.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It's okay if I did,
okay, great.

00:24:47.351 --> 00:24:49.640
Well, the point is, what they
did is, they basically tried to

00:24:49.641 --> 00:24:53.170
figure what caused developers
to believe things, right?

00:24:53.171 --> 00:24:54.259
What did they believe,

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:56.986
and they categorized it in
a whole bunch of different ways.

00:24:56.987 --> 00:24:57.814
And they found, and

00:24:57.815 --> 00:24:59.960
this relates to previous
social science research,

00:24:59.961 --> 00:25:02.166
also something I really
appreciate about the paper.

00:25:02.167 --> 00:25:05.363
That things like personal
experience tend to be a little

00:25:05.364 --> 00:25:08.908
bit more convincing to people
than research papers, right?

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:14.410
And so things like what I hear
from my colleagues or peers or

00:25:14.411 --> 00:25:17.000
managers were little bit
ahead of research papers.

00:25:17.001 --> 00:25:19.040
And there's a couple ways
you can interpret this.

00:25:19.041 --> 00:25:21.247
And you guys were really
cautious in your paper, and

00:25:21.248 --> 00:25:22.809
I will be a little
bit less cautious.

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:26.291
One way that you can interpret
this is that social theories

00:25:26.292 --> 00:25:28.706
seems to indicate,
as far as I can tell,

00:25:28.707 --> 00:25:32.617
that personal relationships
matter and that's probably true,

00:25:32.618 --> 00:25:35.264
don't really have any
objections to that.

00:25:35.265 --> 00:25:39.643
However, we might want to ask,
what is in these research papers

00:25:39.644 --> 00:25:42.899
and what is the evidence
data for these things?

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:46.201
It's at least
a question we can ask.

00:25:46.202 --> 00:25:49.948
And we might ask this because
when we look at people like

00:25:49.949 --> 00:25:53.950
language designers, they make
a ton of testable claims.

00:25:55.540 --> 00:25:58.780
So for example,
it's a little hard to see, but

00:25:58.781 --> 00:26:02.720
you might believe that Ruby is a
dynamic, open source programming

00:26:02.721 --> 00:26:04.999
language that focuses on
simplicity and productivity.

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:07.436
That's what they say.

00:26:07.437 --> 00:26:10.797
We're not saying it's true or
false, is a library,

00:26:10.798 --> 00:26:11.710
not a language.

00:26:11.711 --> 00:26:12.770
But you can build powerful,

00:26:12.771 --> 00:26:16.060
concurrent distributed
applications more easily.

00:26:16.061 --> 00:26:18.890
More easily is a testable plan,
and you have to define it,

00:26:18.891 --> 00:26:22.570
but it is something that could
be false and it could be true.

00:26:24.470 --> 00:26:28.220
Perl is flexible and
powerful, cue for laughter.

00:26:28.221 --> 00:26:31.950
And the point is, not so

00:26:31.951 --> 00:26:35.000
much the language designers
are right or wrong, it's that,

00:26:35.001 --> 00:26:38.570
people clearly have beliefs and
there's also marketing claims.

00:26:38.571 --> 00:26:40.670
So it's a complicated situation,
of course you have to note,

00:26:40.671 --> 00:26:42.490
there's no problem with saying,

00:26:42.491 --> 00:26:44.160
I wanna get my tools out
there and get them used.

00:26:44.161 --> 00:26:46.140
That's totally normal,
that's not a big deal.

00:26:46.141 --> 00:26:47.830
But on the other hand,

00:26:47.831 --> 00:26:49.750
that's the job of
the business community.

00:26:49.751 --> 00:26:51.640
The job of the scholarly
community is to figure out

00:26:51.641 --> 00:26:54.790
what's true, to figure out
something is true or false.

00:26:55.870 --> 00:26:59.310
My job, I don't care about
marketing, I'm an academic, so

00:26:59.311 --> 00:27:00.750
my job's a little different.

00:27:00.751 --> 00:27:04.790
And as it turns out, in addition
to the research papers,

00:27:04.791 --> 00:27:07.070
developers are somewhat
skeptical of research papers or

00:27:07.071 --> 00:27:08.390
at least it appears so.

00:27:08.391 --> 00:27:09.710
It's my impression
from the data and

00:27:09.711 --> 00:27:12.450
you can disagree if I
got that wrong, but

00:27:12.451 --> 00:27:14.570
I don't think you'd, yeah, see?

00:27:14.571 --> 00:27:16.185
Okay, there we go.

00:27:16.186 --> 00:27:17.383
So educators, as well,

00:27:17.384 --> 00:27:20.292
people with direct expertise
in computer science education

00:27:20.293 --> 00:27:23.381
actually don't understand the
problems their students have.

00:27:23.382 --> 00:27:27.148
Wonderful, wonderful paper that
was just published by Brown and

00:27:27.149 --> 00:27:27.839
Altadmri.

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:30.845
He collected 100 million
compiler errors.

00:27:30.846 --> 00:27:31.805
So I'm gonna go
out on a limb and

00:27:31.806 --> 00:27:33.579
say that's a sufficient
sample size.

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:38.400
And if you don't know any of
Neil Brown's work, it's amazing.

00:27:38.401 --> 00:27:40.430
It's just absolutely fantastic,

00:27:40.431 --> 00:27:43.530
scholarly, tour de
force type stuff.

00:27:43.531 --> 00:27:45.875
And one of the things he
did in his recent paper was

00:27:45.876 --> 00:27:48.080
[LAUGH] really made me laugh.

00:27:48.081 --> 00:27:51.640
He basically asked top
conferences in computer science

00:27:51.641 --> 00:27:55.426
and education like Acer but
also sort of local UK teachers,

00:27:55.427 --> 00:27:58.378
and sort of like
a group of educators,

00:27:58.379 --> 00:28:00.363
but not necessarily experts.

00:28:00.364 --> 00:28:02.738
They might teach computer
science or do other things, but

00:28:02.739 --> 00:28:05.222
in theory, they at least have
some connection to students.

00:28:05.223 --> 00:28:08.090
But what he found was that
you can look at the data and

00:28:08.091 --> 00:28:11.890
there are weak correlations,
but they're very weak.

00:28:11.891 --> 00:28:16.758
Basically, if you actually track
data and then you go ask experts

00:28:16.759 --> 00:28:21.020
what they think the answer is,
they really have no idea.

00:28:21.021 --> 00:28:23.980
Or at least, a very weak
association with the truth.

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:30.072
And this turns out there's
at least some evidence.

00:28:30.073 --> 00:28:32.723
I think that this paper is far
less compelling in terms of its

00:28:32.724 --> 00:28:34.367
size of its data,
but nonetheless,

00:28:34.368 --> 00:28:36.685
it's an interesting paper and
I wanna mention it.

00:28:36.686 --> 00:28:40.045
There's a lot of work that's
gone on over the years on

00:28:40.046 --> 00:28:42.444
Unified Modeling Language,
or UML.

00:28:42.445 --> 00:28:43.846
Just show of hands,

00:28:43.847 --> 00:28:46.738
how many people use
UML in here every day?

00:28:46.739 --> 00:28:51.258
Yeah, this data, that's zero,
just for all those watching on

00:28:51.259 --> 00:28:55.710
the old Internet, the data from
Marian Petry also shows this.

00:28:55.711 --> 00:28:58.731
It basically says, we spend a
lot of time in academia talking

00:28:58.732 --> 00:29:01.516
about the details of these
models and looking at them and

00:29:01.517 --> 00:29:04.600
thinking about them and making
little theories about them and

00:29:04.601 --> 00:29:06.637
stuff like that, and
nobody uses them.

00:29:06.638 --> 00:29:09.260
There are a handful of
developers in her sample that

00:29:09.261 --> 00:29:10.470
did it a little.

00:29:10.471 --> 00:29:11.674
But generally speaking,

00:29:11.675 --> 00:29:13.374
developers don't
use it that much.

00:29:13.375 --> 00:29:17.124
&gt;&gt; I think there's a certain
[INAUDIBLE] with groups,

00:29:17.125 --> 00:29:21.482
I mean so IBM's Rational has
a lot of customers and they're

00:29:21.483 --> 00:29:26.210
printing courses for [INAUDIBLE]
people are quite happy with it.

00:29:26.211 --> 00:29:27.590
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you and
I are in total agreement.

00:29:27.591 --> 00:29:31.272
I mean, that's why I said, this
is a sample size of 50 people

00:29:31.273 --> 00:29:33.320
that Marian Petry went and
asked.

00:29:33.321 --> 00:29:34.600
Are those the right 50 people?

00:29:34.601 --> 00:29:36.260
I have no idea.

00:29:36.261 --> 00:29:39.110
But nonetheless, I think it's at
least a data point to consider,

00:29:39.111 --> 00:29:40.790
even if I don't have
a better sample.

00:29:40.791 --> 00:29:43.710
But yeah, you're completely
right, that's totally true.

00:29:43.711 --> 00:29:45.500
Probably depends on
context in organization

00:29:45.501 --> 00:29:46.100
and stuff like that.

00:29:48.770 --> 00:29:50.610
Then we can ask the question,

00:29:50.611 --> 00:29:52.370
what is actually in
the research papers?

00:29:52.371 --> 00:29:53.850
Is there data in them, right?

00:29:53.851 --> 00:29:55.710
That's a simple and
reasonable question.

00:29:55.711 --> 00:29:59.420
Did scholars,
people paid to find the truth,

00:29:59.421 --> 00:30:01.770
use the scientific method and
evidence?

00:30:01.771 --> 00:30:05.070
And it turns out for programming
language data, a few years ago,

00:30:05.071 --> 00:30:08.870
we ran a paper and
we looked at workshops.

00:30:08.871 --> 00:30:11.760
So these are venues that
are not considered prestigious.

00:30:11.761 --> 00:30:13.310
They're not the top venues.

00:30:13.311 --> 00:30:16.610
They're just some places that
claim that they've gathered data

00:30:16.611 --> 00:30:17.520
on these issues.

00:30:17.521 --> 00:30:18.350
There's three of them.

00:30:18.351 --> 00:30:20.080
One was called Empirical Studies
of Programmers.

00:30:20.081 --> 00:30:21.580
That no longer exists.

00:30:21.581 --> 00:30:22.907
There's another one called PPIG.

00:30:22.908 --> 00:30:23.670
It's out of the UK.

00:30:23.671 --> 00:30:26.058
A lot of Cambridge people were
doing it for a long time.

00:30:26.059 --> 00:30:29.157
And there's another one called
which I actually participate in

00:30:29.158 --> 00:30:30.096
and I like.

00:30:30.097 --> 00:30:32.766
But nonetheless, it's reasonable
to ask what the evidence

00:30:32.767 --> 00:30:34.031
standard is at such a venue.

00:30:34.032 --> 00:30:36.879
And it turns out if you track
the data very carefully,

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:40.600
generally speaking, there
wasn't really much evidence.

00:30:40.601 --> 00:30:43.390
And when I say very much,
if you read the details of this,

00:30:43.391 --> 00:30:45.378
you find that it's
astonishingly low.

00:30:45.379 --> 00:30:49.564
Even simple things like,
did they try to detect fraud,

00:30:49.565 --> 00:30:52.690
would not be included
in studies on that.

00:30:52.691 --> 00:30:55.080
Now I thought I was crazy
when we found this,

00:30:55.081 --> 00:30:58.000
cuz we ended up tracking a lot
more papers than was in this.

00:30:58.001 --> 00:31:02.162
We ended up with about a data
set of about 2,200 papers.

00:31:02.163 --> 00:31:03.993
And we thought that
we were crazy,

00:31:03.994 --> 00:31:07.403
because a lot of the papers that
were published at major venues,

00:31:07.404 --> 00:31:08.729
small venues, that is,

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:12.760
dedicated to certain tasks
weren't really using evidence.

00:31:12.761 --> 00:31:15.960
And so I thought that was insane
until this dissertation came out

00:31:15.961 --> 00:31:18.290
by a guy name
Antti Juhani Kaijanaho.

00:31:18.291 --> 00:31:20.767
Now, let me set the stage for
a second.

00:31:20.768 --> 00:31:22.067
In the medical sciences,

00:31:22.068 --> 00:31:24.499
which is very different
than computer science.

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:28.424
Between 1960 and 2000, the use
of these sorta fraud prevention

00:31:28.425 --> 00:31:31.318
procedures, which were
developed for good reason,

00:31:31.319 --> 00:31:34.856
increased past 1960 by 11.2%
a year after this guy named

00:31:34.857 --> 00:31:37.410
Austin Bradford Hill
came into play.

00:31:37.411 --> 00:31:38.640
We won't go into the details,

00:31:38.641 --> 00:31:41.460
although they
are insanely fascinating.

00:31:41.461 --> 00:31:44.190
He's the guy that figured out
that smoking causes lung cancer,

00:31:44.191 --> 00:31:46.250
if you don't know,
Bradford Hill.

00:31:46.251 --> 00:31:49.270
Increases it by 2,500% if
you smoke a couple packs,

00:31:49.271 --> 00:31:50.760
I mean a couple a day.

00:31:50.761 --> 00:31:53.986
Anyway, the point is,
compared to those procedures,

00:31:53.987 --> 00:31:57.490
they ended up with something
like 110,000 studies or

00:31:57.491 --> 00:31:59.220
somewhere in that ballpark.

00:31:59.221 --> 00:32:04.498
By the end of 2012, we had 22.

00:32:04.499 --> 00:32:07.879
So this includes top venues,
OOPSLA,

00:32:07.880 --> 00:32:11.663
major academic venues that
are dedicated to program and

00:32:11.664 --> 00:32:16.253
languages, software engineering,
any topic that you would think

00:32:16.254 --> 00:32:20.360
might be relevant when we're
talking about wages in the size

00:32:20.361 --> 00:32:23.280
of $330 billion in
the United States.

00:32:23.281 --> 00:32:26.058
22 experiments, and
when I say 22 experiments,

00:32:26.059 --> 00:32:28.040
I mean something specific.

00:32:28.041 --> 00:32:30.480
This is what Kaijanaho
considered to be randomized

00:32:30.481 --> 00:32:31.390
controlled trials.

00:32:31.391 --> 00:32:34.080
And that matters because these
are fraud prevention procedures.

00:32:34.081 --> 00:32:36.798
These are like,
you didn't try to cheat,

00:32:36.799 --> 00:32:38.920
you had a control group.

00:32:38.921 --> 00:32:40.110
If you said Ruby was good,

00:32:40.111 --> 00:32:44.090
you compared it to something and
you didn't test yourself.

00:32:44.091 --> 00:32:47.540
Just really, really basic stuff.

00:32:47.541 --> 00:32:50.350
Now you might think this doesn't
matter until you start looking

00:32:50.351 --> 00:32:53.040
at some other data from Andy Ko,
who's

00:32:53.041 --> 00:32:54.659
at the University of Washington,
if you don't know him.

00:32:56.060 --> 00:32:59.570
They analyzed four major
top conferences or

00:32:59.571 --> 00:33:01.820
journals in software
engineering,

00:33:01.821 --> 00:33:04.150
and they found something
also sort of odd.

00:33:04.151 --> 00:33:07.200
On the one hand,
they did find that 77% of

00:33:07.201 --> 00:33:10.460
papers do include some
kinda empirical evaluation.

00:33:10.461 --> 00:33:12.448
So people are using some
kind of data of some kind.

00:33:12.449 --> 00:33:16.237
However, even though software
engineering and in theory,

00:33:16.238 --> 00:33:19.952
that's connected to something
related to people building

00:33:19.953 --> 00:33:23.609
a software, only 27% evaluated
human use of the tool.

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:26.833
And of those,
62% of them tested themselves.

00:33:26.834 --> 00:33:28.115
Right?

00:33:28.116 --> 00:33:32.315
They said I have this great
tool, I tested it on me,

00:33:32.316 --> 00:33:33.986
and it was kick ass.

00:33:33.987 --> 00:33:34.769
That sought of idea.

00:33:34.770 --> 00:33:35.950
Now this isn't my data.

00:33:35.951 --> 00:33:39.127
This is something I've
confirmed [LAUGH] with Andy,

00:33:39.128 --> 00:33:41.010
because I didn't believe it.

00:33:41.011 --> 00:33:44.034
That's insane, that's, there's
no way that should be true.

00:33:44.035 --> 00:33:47.854
There's no way that should
make a pass pure review, but

00:33:47.855 --> 00:33:50.713
62% of the time
that's what happens.

00:33:50.714 --> 00:33:51.365
Yeah?

00:33:51.366 --> 00:33:53.340
&gt;&gt; What do these
numbers look like in?

00:33:53.341 --> 00:33:55.410
&gt;&gt; I have no idea,
it's never been tested, but

00:33:55.411 --> 00:33:58.110
I bet it'd be better,
but I don't know.

00:33:58.111 --> 00:34:00.674
&gt;&gt; The culture is to use
a team of studies, and

00:34:00.675 --> 00:34:03.808
more, I guess methodology
comes from psychology,

00:34:03.809 --> 00:34:06.550
than we do in
software engineering.

00:34:06.551 --> 00:34:09.261
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would bet that
that'd be true as well, but

00:34:09.262 --> 00:34:11.354
I've never seen a meta
analysis on it.

00:34:11.355 --> 00:34:13.714
&gt;&gt; Are you gonna take a position
on natural experiments?

00:34:13.715 --> 00:34:17.078
Do you have a feeling of what
natural experiments are at, and

00:34:17.079 --> 00:34:19.871
basically what people would
get out of this for example?

00:34:19.872 --> 00:34:22.111
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm happy to
talk about that, and

00:34:22.112 --> 00:34:25.069
I know you're an MSR guy type,
that kind of stuff too.

00:34:25.070 --> 00:34:25.764
I love them.

00:34:25.765 --> 00:34:26.418
I think they're great.

00:34:26.419 --> 00:34:31.126
I actually use your research in
my lab for generics because-

00:34:31.127 --> 00:34:32.450
&gt;&gt; I hope.

00:34:32.451 --> 00:34:34.590
&gt;&gt; It does,
actually it turns out, okay, so

00:34:34.591 --> 00:34:37.590
let me explain this in
the context of medical history.

00:34:37.591 --> 00:34:39.943
So in the 1940s,
this isn't on the slides, but

00:34:39.944 --> 00:34:42.508
I know this off the top of my
head, cuz this is what I like,

00:34:42.509 --> 00:34:43.432
this is what I love.

00:34:43.433 --> 00:34:46.526
In the 1940s, when people were
coming back from World War II,

00:34:46.527 --> 00:34:49.289
there was this huge opportunity
in the medical community,

00:34:49.290 --> 00:34:51.168
because soldiers were
coming back, and

00:34:51.169 --> 00:34:52.950
they had very serious problems.

00:34:52.951 --> 00:34:55.080
These problems were really
serious to the point

00:34:55.081 --> 00:34:58.710
where the nation go together and
said, we have to make sure we're

00:34:58.711 --> 00:35:02.100
doing the best we can for
the soldiers as possible.

00:35:02.101 --> 00:35:04.880
The only way they could get the
kind of sample sizes that people

00:35:04.881 --> 00:35:07.130
believed was through the VA.

00:35:07.131 --> 00:35:08.938
But there was [LAUGH] a problem.

00:35:08.939 --> 00:35:12.140
It turns out the actual modern
way of conducting control

00:35:12.141 --> 00:35:15.291
is only conduct finished in 1935
with Ronald Fisher's famous book

00:35:15.292 --> 00:35:18.127
on experimental design, which
discussed like p-values, and

00:35:18.128 --> 00:35:20.855
all that kind of stuff, and we
all know that there's problems

00:35:20.856 --> 00:35:22.610
here and
there with that kind of stuff.

00:35:22.611 --> 00:35:27.006
But nonetheless, in the 40s what
was happening is the VA started

00:35:27.007 --> 00:35:29.126
doing sort of experiments, and

00:35:29.127 --> 00:35:31.649
they couldn't really
do it at scale.

00:35:31.650 --> 00:35:34.528
They'd go to this one hospital
and the doctor would be like,

00:35:34.529 --> 00:35:36.673
I don't believe in
control groups, sorry,

00:35:36.674 --> 00:35:39.722
and then you'd go to this other
