00:41:37.006 --> 00:41:40.960
So number one, if we talk about
students, now when I say this I

00:41:40.961 --> 00:41:44.552
always like to talk about
students and professionals.

00:41:44.553 --> 00:41:47.697
There's a really good reason for
this, and that is because People

00:41:47.698 --> 00:41:49.842
program at different
periods of their life.

00:41:49.843 --> 00:41:52.963
There are students in K through
12 that are learning programing

00:41:52.964 --> 00:41:54.372
in kindergarten.

00:41:54.373 --> 00:41:57.230
So it's completely reasonable
to ask how hard is it for

00:41:57.231 --> 00:41:58.330
a kindergartener.

00:41:58.331 --> 00:41:59.140
On the other hand, and

00:41:59.141 --> 00:42:02.150
this is something Andy said in
one of his papers once actually,

00:42:02.151 --> 00:42:03.760
Andy said but

00:42:03.761 --> 00:42:06.100
people in industry actually
invent the technologies.

00:42:06.101 --> 00:42:07.898
That's obviously true, right?

00:42:07.899 --> 00:42:10.970
It seems like Microsoft builds
tools that get used by people.

00:42:10.971 --> 00:42:13.490
And if we don't take
them into account either,

00:42:13.491 --> 00:42:15.060
that seems spurious, right?

00:42:15.061 --> 00:42:17.380
Why wouldn't we just
take both into account?

00:42:17.381 --> 00:42:19.310
And then look at what
the data says as a whole.

00:42:19.311 --> 00:42:22.830
Again, it's coherence,
right, this idea.

00:42:22.831 --> 00:42:25.703
And so one of the things that
we like do when we do student

00:42:25.704 --> 00:42:28.763
studies, is we like to sorta
tease out what things might be

00:42:28.764 --> 00:42:32.012
impacting them, positive or
negative, either on average or

00:42:32.013 --> 00:42:32.711
for groups.

00:42:32.712 --> 00:42:35.700
And you'll see that grouping
matters in this context.

00:42:35.701 --> 00:42:39.114
So one of the studies we did
a few years ago was on language

00:42:39.115 --> 00:42:40.941
notation or syntax, right?

00:42:40.942 --> 00:42:41.972
Now when I say syntax,

00:42:41.973 --> 00:42:44.906
I use that term loosely because
computer scientists often think

00:42:44.907 --> 00:42:46.709
about it like this is a parser,
right?

00:42:46.710 --> 00:42:48.340
This is not what I mean.

00:42:48.341 --> 00:42:50.960
What I mean is that different
programming languages have used

00:42:50.961 --> 00:42:53.690
different notations to
mean different semantics.

00:42:53.691 --> 00:42:55.850
And those can be done
in multiple ways.

00:42:55.851 --> 00:42:57.370
Does that matter?

00:42:57.371 --> 00:43:00.110
And it turns out,
one way that we did that is,

00:43:00.111 --> 00:43:05.050
at least at the time, I grabbed
one particular language

00:43:05.051 --> 00:43:08.290
that we made with rolling dice,
which we came up with.

00:43:08.291 --> 00:43:10.860
Because of looking at Benjamin
Franklin's experiments from

00:43:10.861 --> 00:43:13.040
the late 1700s on mesmerism,

00:43:13.041 --> 00:43:14.960
which was just another
fraud technique.

00:43:14.961 --> 00:43:17.260
And so what we did is I had my
graduate students sit around and

00:43:17.261 --> 00:43:18.630
use a random number
generator and

00:43:18.631 --> 00:43:21.330
generate a programming
language totally randomly.

00:43:21.331 --> 00:43:25.400
The symbols were slash for
this and plus for

00:43:25.401 --> 00:43:27.440
that and it's just random
symbols from the ASCII table.

00:43:27.441 --> 00:43:29.170
And you have to try
to interpret that.

00:43:29.171 --> 00:43:32.210
And we did that because we were
thinking that might give us

00:43:32.211 --> 00:43:33.820
a baseline for languages.

00:43:33.821 --> 00:43:35.930
It can't possibly be the case,

00:43:35.931 --> 00:43:39.370
that languages aren't at
least as easy to understand

00:43:39.371 --> 00:43:41.520
as one where we used
a random number generator.

00:43:41.521 --> 00:43:43.860
So we called it a placebo
language, right.

00:43:43.861 --> 00:43:44.990
Now, is placebo the right word?

00:43:44.991 --> 00:43:46.120
You can debate it.

00:43:46.121 --> 00:43:48.230
In the literature
in the late 1700s,

00:43:48.231 --> 00:43:50.130
they called them
sham treatments.

00:43:50.131 --> 00:43:53.360
And so whether you call it
placebo or a sham is relevant.

00:43:53.361 --> 00:43:54.350
But that's the idea.

00:43:54.351 --> 00:43:57.400
It's something we
shouldn't see work, right?

00:43:57.401 --> 00:43:59.110
Or that you will expect
people to do poorly.

00:43:59.111 --> 00:44:02.219
And that's surprisingly people
do very poorly when they're just

00:44:02.220 --> 00:44:05.003
starting out with the language
that is designed randomly.

00:44:05.004 --> 00:44:06.450
That's not as surprising.

00:44:06.451 --> 00:44:08.680
What was more surprising
was that Perl and

00:44:08.681 --> 00:44:12.030
Java didn't do any better,
and we know why.

00:44:13.280 --> 00:44:16.465
The reason why is because the
specific word choices that we're

00:44:16.466 --> 00:44:19.169
able to pull out using
a statistical procedure called

00:44:19.170 --> 00:44:20.502
token accuracy mapping.

00:44:20.503 --> 00:44:23.970
And it's really simple,
it works like this.

00:44:23.971 --> 00:44:27.090
You basically take the examples
that people gave you and

00:44:27.091 --> 00:44:29.130
they have to be small and
there's some rules about it.

00:44:29.131 --> 00:44:30.900
And you have to use these
particular techniques to

00:44:30.901 --> 00:44:31.640
evaluate it.

00:44:31.641 --> 00:44:34.640
And you have to check
reliability between independent

00:44:34.641 --> 00:44:36.598
observers of these techniques
and stuff like that.

00:44:36.599 --> 00:44:39.050
And that stuff has to
be hammered out and

00:44:39.051 --> 00:44:40.290
correct to do it right.

00:44:40.291 --> 00:44:41.020
But if you do that,

00:44:41.021 --> 00:44:44.720
you can actually see the areas
of a programming language

00:44:44.721 --> 00:44:46.970
that novices did or
did not do correctly.

00:44:46.971 --> 00:44:49.520
And it turns out,
a lot of the things we take for

00:44:49.521 --> 00:44:54.720
granted like for in a loop
mean nothing to novices.

00:44:54.721 --> 00:44:57.140
So for example, if you use
the word repeat over for,

00:44:57.141 --> 00:44:59.710
it triples their accuracy
on that token, triple.

00:45:00.830 --> 00:45:03.450
If you use equals instead
of double equals, and

00:45:03.451 --> 00:45:06.310
you'll overload it for
both the assignment and

00:45:06.311 --> 00:45:09.580
equality, which you think might
be bad, I thought was bad.

00:45:09.581 --> 00:45:13.055
It turns out, it has no impact
on assignment and it gives you

00:45:13.056 --> 00:45:17.780
an increase on the equality some
of them eight fold in accuracy.

00:45:17.781 --> 00:45:19.650
And when you think about it,
that kinda makes sense.

00:45:19.651 --> 00:45:21.000
It's misleading, right,

00:45:21.001 --> 00:45:24.750
to a novice that hasn't
already been trained, right?

00:45:24.751 --> 00:45:27.110
Now you might also think,
this is a small study.

00:45:27.111 --> 00:45:30.190
This study was replicated,
I don't know, a bunch of times,

00:45:30.191 --> 00:45:33.000
and then we ended up publishing
two of those replications.

00:45:33.001 --> 00:45:34.630
One of them was small
scale with 18 people,

00:45:34.631 --> 00:45:37.030
the other had 60 something,
right?

00:45:37.031 --> 00:45:38.650
Same answer for both, right?

00:45:38.651 --> 00:45:40.540
Different samples
all that stuff.

00:45:40.541 --> 00:45:41.290
But you might think,

00:45:41.291 --> 00:45:44.400
if we did a natural experiment
like we look at Neil Brown's

00:45:44.401 --> 00:45:47.890
data, maybe we don't get
the same answer, but we do.

00:45:49.280 --> 00:45:53.079
In fact, it turns out that the
regions token accuracy mapping

00:45:53.080 --> 00:45:56.440
predicts would be hard for
novices are exactly the same

00:45:56.441 --> 00:45:59.601
things we find in these
massive natural datasets.

00:45:59.602 --> 00:46:00.550
They get the same answer.

00:46:01.810 --> 00:46:05.052
But, and this is an important
but, this is novices, right?

00:46:05.053 --> 00:46:08.880
This doesn't mean this will help
you, right, not necessarily.

00:46:08.881 --> 00:46:11.870
So what we need to do is we
need to also ask questions that

00:46:11.871 --> 00:46:13.560
look at different
kinds of people.

00:46:13.561 --> 00:46:16.580
So one of the ones that we've
kind of had fun getting hate

