But at the end of the day,
static typing allow these

00:47:01.491 --> 00:47:05.260
individuals to write correct
programs defined operationally

00:47:05.261 --> 00:47:07.710
by the paper more quickly,
right?

00:47:07.711 --> 00:47:09.024
And we know why.

00:47:09.025 --> 00:47:12.520
And the reason why is because
developers have dynamic typing.

00:47:12.521 --> 00:47:15.803
What they do when they want to
use a function is to go look for

00:47:15.804 --> 00:47:17.616
what they pass to the function.

00:47:17.617 --> 00:47:20.884
And if you videotape them and
you take out that time,

00:47:20.885 --> 00:47:21.710
it matches.

00:47:23.070 --> 00:47:24.830
In other words,
if you have dynamic typing and

00:47:24.831 --> 00:47:25.590
you wanna use a function,

00:47:25.591 --> 00:47:27.790
you have to know what
the passed to it.

00:47:27.791 --> 00:47:30.980
And we thought, maybe we could
throw that effect away if we

00:47:30.981 --> 00:47:33.480
just put in the documentation
what you passed to it.

00:47:33.481 --> 00:47:35.148
Turns out that doesn't work. Why?

00:47:35.149 --> 00:47:36.366
I don't know but

00:47:36.367 --> 00:47:37.933
that's the answer.

00:47:37.934 --> 00:47:41.069
And then when you look at these
things, one thing that people

00:47:41.070 --> 00:47:44.146
often concern about with
experiments is replication, and

00:47:44.147 --> 00:47:45.860
for good reason.

00:47:45.861 --> 00:47:47.130
Because replication is hard.

00:47:47.131 --> 00:47:48.690
And unfortunately as well,

00:47:48.691 --> 00:47:51.400
other disciplines have things
called evidence standards.

00:47:51.401 --> 00:47:52.576
And this is a technical term,

00:47:52.577 --> 00:47:55.050
this is not something
I'm making up.

00:47:55.051 --> 00:47:59.010
Evidence standard is a formal
document adopted by a community

00:47:59.011 --> 00:48:01.950
that says, you must follow
these particular standards.

00:48:01.951 --> 00:48:04.470
A good example would be
if you have statistics,

00:48:04.471 --> 00:48:05.830
you must report the p-value and

00:48:05.831 --> 00:48:08.190
the effect size would be
a common example, right?

00:48:08.191 --> 00:48:08.830
And they just says,

00:48:08.831 --> 00:48:11.300
you don't get published
unless you have that, right?

00:48:11.301 --> 00:48:14.270
Or another one might be, you
must have a control word, right?

00:48:14.271 --> 00:48:15.240
It's something very simple.

00:48:15.241 --> 00:48:18.090
You can decide as a community
for this particular venue or

00:48:18.091 --> 00:48:19.080
this journal.

00:48:19.081 --> 00:48:21.800
If you don't at least have this
fraud prevention procedure in

00:48:21.801 --> 00:48:23.840
place, you won't get published.

00:48:23.841 --> 00:48:26.670
The most common one in
medicine is called CONSORT.

00:48:26.671 --> 00:48:30.910
And this one was designed in
part because of all sorts of

00:48:30.911 --> 00:48:32.380
problems in the 60s.

00:48:32.381 --> 00:48:35.240
But also because of some
famous court cases in the 2000

00:48:35.241 --> 00:48:37.780
where corporations were
committing fraud that was

00:48:37.781 --> 00:48:39.790
murdering people, right?

00:48:39.791 --> 00:48:42.135
Which is not pleasant to say but
it's true.

00:48:42.136 --> 00:48:44.352
So they have this things called
Register Randomize Control

00:48:44.353 --> 00:48:47.400
Trials, it's a fraud prevention
technique, etc, etc.

00:48:47.401 --> 00:48:50.350
So, we try, even though I
work in a discipline that

00:48:50.351 --> 00:48:51.650
has no evidence standards for

00:48:51.651 --> 00:48:54.680
these things,
I have evidence standards.

00:48:54.681 --> 00:48:57.880
And so one thing that we do is
we replicate our experiments in

00:48:57.881 --> 00:48:59.970
all sorts of different ways.

00:48:59.971 --> 00:49:02.490
So for example, are statically
typed APIs faster to use?

00:49:02.491 --> 00:49:05.410
This is two studies, I mentioned
at the top, the answer was yes.

00:49:05.411 --> 00:49:08.276
They had the same effect size,
effectively, the same answer,

00:49:08.277 --> 00:49:09.186
different people.

00:49:09.187 --> 00:49:11.990
Sometimes different countries,
right?

00:49:11.991 --> 00:49:13.880
Is the previous finding
only a matter of syntax?

00:49:13.881 --> 00:49:17.360
Turns out if you twiddle with
the type declarations to make

00:49:17.361 --> 00:49:19.110
them wrong, then yes,

00:49:19.111 --> 00:49:23.053
it's misleading, and
that's what happens, right.

00:49:23.054 --> 00:49:25.860
Can documentation compensate for

00:49:25.861 --> 00:49:27.130
the positive effect
of certain types?

00:49:27.131 --> 00:49:30.340
Turns out the answer is no,
it has a small impact but

00:49:30.341 --> 00:49:31.500
the effect size is only 0.14, so

00:49:31.501 --> 00:49:32.780
it's not that big
of a difference.

00:49:34.150 --> 00:49:35.960
Do generics help
with static typing?

00:49:35.961 --> 00:49:37.030
Turns out, yes, but

00:49:37.031 --> 00:49:39.820
only in certain regions
of the code, right?

00:49:39.821 --> 00:49:42.720
So it turns out if you have
generics on method declarations,

00:49:42.721 --> 00:49:43.840
that tends to help.

00:49:43.841 --> 00:49:45.130
But if you have to use and

00:49:45.131 --> 00:49:49.680
write your own generics classes,
that gets nasty, right?

00:49:49.681 --> 00:49:50.645
What about IDEs?

00:49:50.646 --> 00:49:52.722
So some of the previous studies
were done in text editors only.

00:49:52.723 --> 00:49:55.153
It could be that just doing it
in a text editor is the problem,

00:49:55.154 --> 00:49:57.395
and that if you just had really
good IDE support that that

00:49:57.396 --> 00:49:57.933
would help.

00:49:57.934 --> 00:49:59.297
Turns out, it doesn't,

00:49:59.298 --> 00:50:02.778
same effect size regardless of
having a really up-to-date IDE.

00:50:02.779 --> 00:50:07.419
What about a benefit for dynamic
type systems if you just to go

00:50:07.420 --> 00:50:11.312
totally nuts and
you try to design static typing?

00:50:11.313 --> 00:50:15.409
Well it's insane, you just come
up with the craziest examples

00:50:15.410 --> 00:50:19.275
where you have to have triply
nested generics with question

00:50:19.276 --> 00:50:21.929
mark extends or
some other nasty stuff.

00:50:21.930 --> 00:50:23.564
It turns out if you do that,

00:50:23.565 --> 00:50:27.260
there is a way to see a small
benefit from dynamic typing.

00:50:27.261 --> 00:50:30.172
But it's so esoteric that it's
hard to see most developers

00:50:30.173 --> 00:50:33.375
actually doing that in practice,
although you could verify that

00:50:33.376 --> 00:50:35.369
with a natural experiment,
all right?

00:50:35.370 --> 00:50:39.430
In fact, you did, which is why
we did it that way, right?

00:50:39.431 --> 00:50:42.497
And then finally this one
isn't published yet, but

00:50:42.498 --> 00:50:45.355
some people argue if only
we had type inference,

00:50:45.356 --> 00:50:47.305
a lot of work has
gone at Poppel and

00:50:47.306 --> 00:50:50.323
places like that to do type
inferred type systems.

00:50:50.324 --> 00:50:52.823
It could be that that
inference is what helps, but

00:50:52.824 --> 00:50:54.630
remember the theory here.

00:50:54.631 --> 00:50:56.550
The theory is that if you
don't know what to pass,

00:50:56.551 --> 00:50:57.754
that's the cause at the time.

00:50:57.755 --> 00:51:01.325
So we would expect to see
no effect and we don't,

00:51:01.326 --> 00:51:03.000
same answer, right?

00:51:03.001 --> 00:51:05.390
So we're talking maybe
10 to 15 replications,

00:51:05.391 --> 00:51:07.358
I forget how many off
the top of my head now.

00:51:07.359 --> 00:51:10.181
And that data seems to match
with people doing natural

00:51:10.182 --> 00:51:12.160
experiments, right?

00:51:12.161 --> 00:51:14.569
So it's not perfect,
but that's where it is.

00:51:14.570 --> 00:51:18.090
So two other examples,
just to give you a taste, right?

00:51:19.420 --> 00:51:22.007
One is, how many people have
heard of aspect-oriented

00:51:22.008 --> 00:51:23.115
programming, right?

00:51:23.116 --> 00:51:24.525
How many believe it's helpful?

00:51:24.526 --> 00:51:27.677
How many believe it's not?

00:51:27.678 --> 00:51:30.206
And how many believe that
it's either unclear or

00:51:30.207 --> 00:51:31.199
you're not sure?

00:51:32.430 --> 00:51:35.754
Okay, so there isn't a lot of
data, there's only a handful of

00:51:35.755 --> 00:51:38.603
experiments, but they seem
to show it has no impact.

00:51:38.604 --> 00:51:40.565
The types of things the guy
that made it, well,

00:51:40.566 --> 00:51:42.490
there's a few people and
they argue about it.

00:51:42.491 --> 00:51:46.149
But one of the people that did
it was Gregor Kiczales back in

00:51:46.150 --> 00:51:46.753
the day.

00:51:46.754 --> 00:51:49.898
And they made arguments saying
basically that it would help for

00:51:49.899 --> 00:51:53.130
certain kinds of code, like
logging and stuff like that.

00:51:53.131 --> 00:51:55.158
And you can find
some effects for

00:51:55.159 --> 00:51:59.376
logging that are kind of small,
but almost nothing else, right?

00:51:59.377 --> 00:52:01.514
So it could be that it's
great for logging, but

00:52:01.515 --> 00:52:03.723
that's just really isn't useful. Otherwise-

00:52:03.724 --> 00:52:05.204
&gt;&gt; That seems to be the only

00:52:05.205 --> 00:52:07.057
argument I've ever heard for

00:52:07.058 --> 00:52:10.432
aspect-oriented is logging
&gt;&gt; And in fact, the data

00:52:10.433 --> 00:52:13.618
shows it's the only one they've
been able to document as true,

00:52:13.619 --> 00:52:16.222
and the effect size is small for
that effect as well.

00:52:16.223 --> 00:52:19.147
I forget which task it was in
here, but it's one of these,

00:52:19.148 --> 00:52:21.297
there's a teeny,
teeny tiny effect, and

00:52:21.298 --> 00:52:22.890
then the rest is just in a wash.

00:52:22.891 --> 00:52:24.980
So your hunch is what's
supported by the data as well.

00:52:24.981 --> 00:52:28.945
So in any case, not much in
the way of replications for

00:52:28.946 --> 00:52:33.265
that experiment but you can
try to invalidate them through

00:52:33.266 --> 00:52:36.987
natural or experimentation
through coherence.

00:52:36.988 --> 00:52:39.371
And the final one is one
that one of my students did,

00:52:39.372 --> 00:52:41.190
I just think it was fun.

00:52:41.191 --> 00:52:45.015
We have heard for years that
lambdas were like the best

00:52:45.016 --> 00:52:47.650
thing in the world
from many people.

00:52:47.651 --> 00:52:49.860
And so we were curious, right?

00:52:49.861 --> 00:52:51.844
So we decided to
test just one thing,

00:52:51.845 --> 00:52:54.024
cuz you can only test
one thing at a time.

00:52:54.025 --> 00:52:56.912
There's no replications on this,
to my knowledge, and

00:52:56.913 --> 00:52:59.090
I don't know of any
natural experiments.

00:52:59.091 --> 00:53:00.131
Maybe they're there or

00:53:00.132 --> 00:53:02.942
maybe there are some pieces in a
couple of papers here and there,

00:53:02.943 --> 00:53:05.404
but like I said, it's not as
much my area of expertise.

00:53:05.405 --> 00:53:08.109
And so what we did
was we thought, okay,

00:53:08.110 --> 00:53:11.620
maybe this is different
across experience levels.

00:53:11.621 --> 00:53:12.823
Maybe you have to be really,

00:53:12.824 --> 00:53:15.241
really experienced with
lambdas to use them correctly.

00:53:15.242 --> 00:53:17.810
Or maybe it's the case
that it'll be the same,

00:53:17.811 --> 00:53:21.580
you get this big effect across
the board, we weren't sure.

00:53:21.581 --> 00:53:23.819
So we tested freshmen in
college, sophomores in college,

00:53:23.820 --> 00:53:25.583
juniors in college, and
seniors in college.

00:53:25.584 --> 00:53:28.460
They learned lambda somewhere
around their junior year or so.

00:53:28.461 --> 00:53:30.405
So they would have known
something about them.

00:53:30.406 --> 00:53:33.511
And for the professionals that
we used, we got people that were

00:53:33.512 --> 00:53:36.808
serious devs that knew something
about functional programming.

00:53:36.809 --> 00:53:39.702
They were self-proclaimed
experts in functional program.

00:53:39.703 --> 00:53:42.230
They know what lambdas are,
there's no debate, right?

00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:47.430
But when you break down the
data, there was no impact for

00:53:47.431 --> 00:53:48.400
the professionals.

00:53:48.401 --> 00:53:51.340
They could solve the tasks about
the same amount of speed as

00:53:51.341 --> 00:53:53.270
using iterator in C++,
all right?

00:53:53.271 --> 00:53:55.845
This is just, again, one
language, I'm not saying C++ has

00:53:55.846 --> 00:53:58.177
the best lambdas, I think
they're kind of atrocious but

00:53:58.178 --> 00:53:59.161
that's not the point.

00:53:59.162 --> 00:54:02.061
Well no, that's not fair,
they're not atrocious, they have

00:54:02.062 --> 00:54:05.080
particular syntactic properties
that I think could be improved.

00:54:06.510 --> 00:54:09.109
There, how's that?

00:54:09.110 --> 00:54:11.302
Well it's true, I mean,
language design is hard and

00:54:11.303 --> 00:54:13.205
sometimes when you're
trying to be general,

00:54:13.206 --> 00:54:15.860
sometimes it's easy to get too
general or not general enough.

00:54:15.861 --> 00:54:17.158
And that's a difficult problem.

00:54:17.159 --> 00:54:21.895
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Conclusions
about lambdas and

00:54:21.896 --> 00:54:26.240
what you did versus
lambdas in C++.

00:54:26.241 --> 00:54:27.866
&gt;&gt; Okay,
that's a great question.

00:54:27.867 --> 00:54:30.360
And the answer's you can't,
right?

00:54:30.361 --> 00:54:33.754
So this particular test only
compared two things, and

00:54:33.755 --> 00:54:36.569
that's C++ lambdas and
C++ iterators.

00:54:36.570 --> 00:54:39.527
So we can't necessarily say that
this would be true in Java,

00:54:39.528 --> 00:54:40.820
other languages.

00:54:40.821 --> 00:54:41.660
And some languages

00:54:41.661 --> 00:54:43.767
have very different ways
of handling lambdas.

00:54:43.768 --> 00:54:46.940
And in fact, they use them in
different contexts, right?

00:54:46.941 --> 00:54:50.375
So for example, the one area
that, if I was to guess where

00:54:50.376 --> 00:54:54.384
lambdas could be really helpful,
there's some really neat stuff

00:54:54.385 --> 00:54:57.409
they do with lambdas in
the JDK for a concurrency.

00:54:57.410 --> 00:55:00.884
I have no idea if this would
be validate in an experiment

00:55:00.885 --> 00:55:01.740
or not.

00:55:01.741 --> 00:55:04.580
But it wouldn't surprise me
if there is a benefit there

00:55:04.581 --> 00:55:06.505
that could be
documented possibly.

00:55:06.506 --> 00:55:09.145
But I could be wrong, I don't
really know cuz we haven't

00:55:09.146 --> 00:55:10.637
tested that particular thing.

00:55:10.638 --> 00:55:14.545
But in comparing C++ to lambdas
versus iterators, we found

00:55:14.546 --> 00:55:18.299
a very, very, strong negative
impact for people with less

00:55:18.300 --> 00:55:22.670
experience compared to doing
a similar thing with iterators.

00:55:22.671 --> 00:55:25.880
And for professionals, we found
no positive impact, right?

00:55:25.881 --> 00:55:28.402
Again, it doesn't mean you
should or shouldn't use lambdas.

00:55:28.403 --> 00:55:30.330
It has nothing to do with that.

00:55:30.331 --> 00:55:33.130
What it means is that there was

00:55:33.131 --> 00:55:35.480
a technical team of people
that spent years and

00:55:35.481 --> 00:55:38.392
years developing a spec and
nobody did a test, right?

00:55:38.393 --> 00:55:40.656
Maybe it's syntax problem and
they should have checked that.

00:55:40.657 --> 00:55:43.818
Or maybe lambdas really don't
have a benefit, that could be

00:55:43.819 --> 00:55:46.796
the case, right, at least
in one particular language.

00:55:46.797 --> 00:55:52.270
But these were added all
over Java, C++, whatever.

00:55:52.271 --> 00:55:55.654
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] People
think that better, okay,

00:55:55.655 --> 00:55:58.360
and before they can
really know what it does

00:55:58.361 --> 00:56:01.231
cuz novices just don't count for
that much.

00:56:01.232 --> 00:56:04.571
In the scheme of things, they
won't cost you $300 billion.

00:56:04.572 --> 00:56:05.348
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:56:05.349 --> 00:56:07.783
&gt;&gt; You have to actually
do it for a language,

00:56:07.784 --> 00:56:12.280
develop a whole of professionals
who are expert at using it.

00:56:12.281 --> 00:56:15.648
And then actually measure it,
and by that point everybody's

00:56:15.649 --> 00:56:18.950
pretty much moved on, except for
the guys who are kinda stuck

00:56:18.951 --> 00:56:21.561
in a belief system that's
part of their world.

00:56:21.562 --> 00:56:25.016
So I don't know what you would
be advocating practically for

00:56:25.017 --> 00:56:26.857
applying what you're saying.

00:56:26.858 --> 00:56:28.650
&gt;&gt; Sure, I'm happy to
answer that, actually.

00:56:28.651 --> 00:56:30.160
So what I'm advocating for

00:56:30.161 --> 00:56:31.990
specifically, I don't
have like a plan for

00:56:31.991 --> 00:56:34.560
action in the slides cuz I'm
not trying to necessarily

00:56:34.561 --> 00:56:36.060
convince you to go do something.

00:56:36.061 --> 00:56:39.778
But what I would say is that
other scientific disciplines

00:56:39.779 --> 00:56:43.587
break down big problems into
manageable chunks, right?

00:56:43.588 --> 00:56:47.656
So for example, medicine,
we have early experiments which

00:56:47.657 --> 00:56:50.500
are either phase one or
before that.

00:56:50.501 --> 00:56:52.660
Where they're really, really
tightly controlled and they try

00:56:52.661 --> 00:56:54.940
to come up with the theory
why something could be true.

00:56:54.941 --> 00:56:58.180
But we all know that just
because you kill a bacteria in a

00:56:58.181 --> 00:57:00.510
lab doesn't mean that you're not
gonna kill the person using it.

00:57:00.511 --> 00:57:01.434
We all know that, right?

00:57:01.435 --> 00:57:02.757
You would agree, right?

00:57:02.758 --> 00:57:06.393
And with computer science,
it needs I think that one path

00:57:06.394 --> 00:57:09.180
forward would be to
do something similar.

00:57:09.181 --> 00:57:11.630
So for example,
if you wanna change C++ for

00:57:11.631 --> 00:57:15.320
the whole world, that could
impact a lot of developers,

00:57:15.321 --> 00:57:16.230
then there's a process.

00:57:16.231 --> 00:57:19.459
You have to have some low level
experiments that at least shows

00:57:19.460 --> 00:57:20.461
it's plausible.

00:57:20.462 --> 00:57:23.322
And then if those worked out, we
need some natural experiments,

00:57:23.323 --> 00:57:25.360
and we need some
experiments over here.

00:57:25.361 --> 00:57:27.993
And then if you go through
this chain, then we say okay,

00:57:27.994 --> 00:57:30.189
the evidence is sufficient,
put it in C++.

00:57:30.190 --> 00:57:34.976
But it seems right now that
the process is just kind of like

00:57:34.977 --> 00:57:38.879
people decide and
they might not do a good job.

00:57:38.880 --> 00:57:41.705
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I don't disagree with,

00:57:41.706 --> 00:57:46.005
my question what you said
other than [INAUDIBLE].

00:57:46.006 --> 00:57:49.382
One thing that does seem like
there might be some hope here is

00:57:49.383 --> 00:57:52.560
if you ignore programming
languages and stuff like that

00:57:52.561 --> 00:57:55.683
will be far less impactful
than features in programs.

00:57:55.684 --> 00:57:59.229
And when people develop
programs, they put features

00:57:59.230 --> 00:58:03.890
in them like my favorite feature
in life is the ribbon in Office.

00:58:03.891 --> 00:58:07.074
And I wonder did that really
improve productivity of people

00:58:07.075 --> 00:58:09.939
who were using Office or
not, all their arguments and

00:58:09.940 --> 00:58:10.676
[INAUDIBLE]?

00:58:10.677 --> 00:58:13.216
And so it makes me wonder if you
could actually practically apply

00:58:13.217 --> 00:58:15.201
this in teaching a software
engineering course.

00:58:15.202 --> 00:58:18.441
That basically said here's a
methodology you can use when you

00:58:18.442 --> 00:58:21.120
put features into a program
you're developing for

00:58:21.121 --> 00:58:23.673
users that would actually
let you know whether or

00:58:23.674 --> 00:58:26.119
not Those features were
a benefit to them now.

00:58:26.120 --> 00:58:27.281
Could you do
something like that?

00:58:27.282 --> 00:58:28.735
&gt;&gt; Yeah, in terms of features,

00:58:28.736 --> 00:58:31.420
I don't think we have any
disagreement there as well.

00:58:33.050 --> 00:58:36.010
And I actually do teach stuff
like this in my classes as well

00:58:36.011 --> 00:58:38.090
for reasons like that.

00:58:38.091 --> 00:58:39.140
For example,
in software engineering,

00:58:39.141 --> 00:58:41.200
I'm not talking about
programming languages.

00:58:41.201 --> 00:58:43.675
But I do want them to at least
understand evidence, for or

00:58:43.676 --> 00:58:44.974
against procedures, yeah.

00:58:44.975 --> 00:58:46.080
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:58:46.081 --> 00:58:49.210
&gt;&gt; A world where people
developed applications people

00:58:49.211 --> 00:58:51.880
use, and actually understand
these kinds of things, and

00:58:51.881 --> 00:58:54.150
actually develop things that
actually will be valuable.

00:58:54.151 --> 00:58:55.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
there's some speculation,

00:58:55.661 --> 00:58:59.140
and I don't know if it's true,
but it would not surprise me.

00:58:59.141 --> 00:59:01.390
Oftentimes in university
education for

00:59:01.391 --> 00:59:05.650
computer science, I hypothesize,
but do not claim to be correct,

00:59:05.651 --> 00:59:08.410
that one of the reasons why
we've done such a poor job.

00:59:08.411 --> 00:59:11.750
And we have really done
a terrible job on using data and

00:59:11.751 --> 00:59:16.150
evidence for many things is
because we don't really require

00:59:16.151 --> 00:59:17.670
any education on it, right?

00:59:17.671 --> 00:59:21.620
Like if you work in some
scientific disciplines you have

00:59:21.621 --> 00:59:22.470
lab hours, right?

00:59:22.471 --> 00:59:24.607
And you have to run
experiments on stuff.

00:59:24.608 --> 00:59:26.760
And we do that with programming.

00:59:26.761 --> 00:59:30.160
We're pretty good I
think as a community of

00:59:30.161 --> 00:59:31.460
trying to make something work.

00:59:31.461 --> 00:59:33.550
Hacking it together and
testing it,

00:59:33.551 --> 00:59:36.830
run on the debugger whatever
your methodology is.

00:59:36.831 --> 00:59:39.760
But when it comes to knowing
these sort of bigger problems

00:59:39.761 --> 00:59:45.060
that actually do impact a lot
of our productivity, that impact

00:59:45.061 --> 00:59:48.700
the tools we use, not just the
languages but the tools in our

00:59:48.701 --> 00:59:51.390
processes and all these things,
less so, right?

00:59:53.470 --> 00:59:56.479
In any case, language wars
is obviously complicated.

00:59:56.480 --> 00:59:59.219
Like I said, this problem is
much bigger than me, it's much

00:59:59.220 --> 01:00:01.906
bigger than all of us, it's
much bigger than one company,

01:00:01.907 --> 01:00:03.325
it's a hard problem, right?

01:00:03.326 --> 01:00:06.407
There's a reason it's
persisted for a long time, but

01:00:06.408 --> 01:00:09.060
it also hasn't been
studied very carefully.

01:00:09.061 --> 01:00:10.370
There's not much data,

01:00:10.371 --> 01:00:12.750
as I think is clear
from the evidence.

01:00:12.751 --> 01:00:14.740
There are some papers here and

01:00:14.741 --> 01:00:17.710
there that are making some
progress or trying to, and we're

01:00:17.711 --> 01:00:19.970
trying to start a community of
people that are doing this.

01:00:19.971 --> 01:00:22.990
So for example we have
Schloss Dagstuhl next year,

01:00:22.991 --> 01:00:24.690
which I'm sure a name
I'm butchering,

01:00:24.691 --> 01:00:27.960
which is basically a group of
academics coming together to try

01:00:27.961 --> 01:00:29.630
to say we need to
make some progress.

01:00:29.631 --> 01:00:32.160
And it needs to actually
use the scientific method.

01:00:32.161 --> 01:00:34.312
So in any case,
thanks for listening and

01:00:34.313 --> 01:00:36.792
I very much appreciate
all of your questions.

01:00:36.793 --> 01:00:44.660
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; More questions?

01:00:44.661 --> 01:00:45.930
&gt;&gt; So one comment.

01:00:45.931 --> 01:00:50.510
So I think the issue here
I think is a cultural one.

01:00:50.511 --> 01:00:53.770
Programming languages
people view programming as

01:00:53.771 --> 01:00:55.520
a mathematical task,
like proving.

01:00:55.521 --> 01:00:57.142
If I'm trying proving something,

01:00:57.143 --> 01:00:59.845
what really matters is the
notation on your definitions and

01:00:59.846 --> 01:01:01.909
your rules of inference,
things like that.

01:01:01.910 --> 01:01:04.299
And whether or not these
notations are accessible to

01:01:04.300 --> 01:01:05.384
hundreds of people, or

01:01:05.385 --> 01:01:08.930
thousands of people who find it
appealing is irrelevant, right?

01:01:08.931 --> 01:01:10.517
And those of us doing
empirical work,

01:01:10.518 --> 01:01:13.560
I think, on the software
engineering side we're sort of

01:01:13.561 --> 01:01:16.590
viewing programming as a human
behavior to be studied as

01:01:16.591 --> 01:01:19.390
a human behavior using
experimental methods.

01:01:19.391 --> 01:01:21.771
So I think these two
disciplines have very different

01:01:21.772 --> 01:01:22.540
perspectives.

01:01:22.541 --> 01:01:24.558
Programming designers don't,
I mean,

01:01:24.559 --> 01:01:27.385
I'm generalizing a bit here so
people who design language

01:01:27.386 --> 01:01:29.870
like Python have a very
different perspective.

01:01:29.871 --> 01:01:34.570
But redefining a language design
like Pascal or TypeScript and so

01:01:34.571 --> 01:01:36.840
on, you're coming from a very
different perspective.

01:01:36.841 --> 01:01:38.860
Which is,
what is the right notation,

01:01:38.861 --> 01:01:40.060
does it have
the right semantics?

01:01:40.061 --> 01:01:41.418
Can I prove the right
properties?

01:01:41.419 --> 01:01:42.801
&gt;&gt; Yeah, yeah, that's true.

01:01:42.802 --> 01:01:45.840
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

01:01:45.841 --> 01:01:47.640
if no human ever used
programming languages,

01:01:47.641 --> 01:01:49.690
I think that that perspective
would be valid, right?

01:01:49.691 --> 01:01:53.510
But you are right, that's
absolutely what's going on,

01:01:53.511 --> 01:01:56.170
is that these communities,
they've existed for a long time,

01:01:56.171 --> 01:01:57.460
and they kinda have their thing.

01:01:57.461 --> 01:01:58.916
They do type
soundness proofs and

01:01:58.917 --> 01:02:00.944
they twiddle with some
semantics and stuff and

01:02:00.945 --> 01:02:02.511
there's nothing wrong with that.

01:02:02.512 --> 01:02:03.581
That's totally okay.

01:02:03.582 --> 01:02:06.865
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

01:02:06.866 --> 01:02:10.199
we have a diverse group,
let's just say that [LAUGH].

01:02:10.200 --> 01:02:13.640
&gt;&gt; Do you have there, too?

01:02:13.641 --> 01:02:14.746
&gt;&gt; Do we have?

01:02:14.747 --> 01:02:17.112
&gt;&gt; Do you have?

01:02:17.113 --> 01:02:18.700
&gt;&gt; Yeah, [LAUGH] for sure.

01:02:18.701 --> 01:02:21.870
Well, we're trying to get a lot
of people outside of computer

01:02:21.871 --> 01:02:22.910
science as well,

01:02:22.911 --> 01:02:26.130
people that understand evidence
standards and stuff like that.

01:02:26.131 --> 01:02:28.480
Well, I've tried to get one,
I don't know if he'll go, but

01:02:28.481 --> 01:02:29.860
in any case.

01:02:29.861 --> 01:02:31.734
At least some people that do,
there's other evidence,

01:02:31.735 --> 01:02:33.780
I'll get to you in
just a second, sorry.

01:02:33.781 --> 01:02:35.389
There's people,
there's other evidence

01:02:35.390 --> 01:02:37.091
standards besides the ones
used in medicine.

01:02:37.092 --> 01:02:40.526
Medicine uses consort, which
is by far the most rigorous of

01:02:40.527 --> 01:02:43.437
anything out there that I
know of for empiricism.

01:02:43.438 --> 01:02:46.235
Cuz if you don't do it right,
you die, right?

01:02:46.236 --> 01:02:47.480
That's kind of a big deal.

01:02:47.481 --> 01:02:50.816
But other communities have
been either adopting consort

01:02:50.817 --> 01:02:54.550
wholesale or they develop
their own because of the law.

01:02:54.551 --> 01:02:55.910
So for example,

01:02:55.911 --> 01:02:58.520
in education they use
the What Works Clearinghouse.

01:02:58.521 --> 01:03:02.050
It's not as rigorous, but it has
fraud prevention standards and

01:03:02.051 --> 01:03:03.870
stuff like that.

01:03:03.871 --> 01:03:05.036
I think he was first and then.

01:03:05.037 --> 01:03:07.533
&gt;&gt; So I just really appreciate
that point because something

01:03:07.534 --> 01:03:09.925
that's always been a mystery
to me is having grown up in

01:03:09.926 --> 01:03:12.526
universities where you have
lots of language designers and

01:03:12.527 --> 01:03:14.936
working with language
designers and safety debates.

01:03:14.937 --> 01:03:17.286
And programming,
that's why I came to this,

01:03:17.287 --> 01:03:19.766
sort of lived through
a lot of programming wars.

01:03:19.767 --> 01:03:23.098
How come all the languages
everybody actually uses

01:03:23.099 --> 01:03:26.817
that are really successful in
terms of broad communities

01:03:26.818 --> 01:03:27.836
are horrible?

01:03:27.837 --> 01:03:31.193
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So actually, the only paper I

01:03:31.194 --> 01:03:34.415
know of that looked at the
adoption in a really rigorous

01:03:34.416 --> 01:03:37.987
way that I would consider as
scientific as we have right now,

01:03:37.988 --> 01:03:42.080
is this wonderful, wonderful
paper by Leo Meyerovich.

01:03:42.081 --> 01:03:43.761
It's this guy that graduated
from Berkeley a bunch of,

01:03:43.762 --> 01:03:44.630
you know this paper, yeah.

01:03:44.631 --> 01:03:45.907
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

01:03:45.908 --> 01:03:47.801
&gt;&gt; Graduated from Berkeley
a bunch of years ago and

01:03:47.802 --> 01:03:48.887
I forget the name of it, but

01:03:48.888 --> 01:03:50.970
it's empirical studies
on language adoptions.

01:03:50.971 --> 01:03:51.557
So I'm happy to send it to you.

01:03:51.558 --> 01:03:54.680
&gt;&gt; Socio-PLT, is that-
&gt;&gt; No no, that's the early one.

01:03:54.681 --> 01:03:57.070
The Socio-PLT paper
is fantastic, but

01:03:57.071 --> 01:03:59.960
the one that had the biggest
dent, I think, was the empirical

01:03:59.961 --> 01:04:01.895
studies on language adoption or
something like that.

01:04:01.896 --> 01:04:04.750
Socio-PLT tried to come
up with a theory, and

01:04:04.751 --> 01:04:07.770
the other one was what
actually occurs, right, yeah.

01:04:07.771 --> 01:04:08.950
Both are good, though.

01:04:08.951 --> 01:04:12.300
But like the Socio-PLT paper had
this fascinating observation

01:04:12.301 --> 01:04:15.540
that religious communities
that are hard to get into,

01:04:15.541 --> 01:04:16.630
people tend to stick with them.

01:04:16.631 --> 01:04:18.810
This is what I was
about to get to.

01:04:18.811 --> 01:04:21.710
It could be the case that's
part of it for languages.

01:04:21.711 --> 01:04:22.620
Like if it's nasty or

01:04:22.621 --> 01:04:25.390
like, I learned it,
I am not doing anything else.

01:04:25.391 --> 01:04:30.018
But I don't think so, I think
that his paper leans more toward

01:04:30.019 --> 01:04:34.735
that it's a social construct,
like if you work at a particular

01:04:34.736 --> 01:04:37.583
company that uses x
you tend to use it.

01:04:37.584 --> 01:04:39.023
If you use
a programming language,

01:04:39.024 --> 01:04:41.140
you tend to use the ones
that you heard about before.

01:04:41.141 --> 01:04:45.131
So for example, if I recall
correctly, and I'm not prepared

01:04:45.132 --> 01:04:48.965
to talk about this paper, but
off the top of my head, I wanna

01:04:48.966 --> 01:04:53.266
say that the strongest predictor
of what language you'll use next

01:04:53.267 --> 01:04:56.835
is the one that you used
previously, stuff like that.

01:04:56.836 --> 01:04:58.622
&gt;&gt; Yeah, those are great
theories, I guess,

01:04:58.623 --> 01:04:59.740
from the scientific side.

01:04:59.741 --> 01:05:03.780
But just observing this
over my career, okay.

01:05:03.781 --> 01:05:06.551
COBOL became what it was
partly because was really,

01:05:06.552 --> 01:05:07.780
really horrible.

01:05:07.781 --> 01:05:10.316
Because businesses said
this is what you did.

01:05:10.317 --> 01:05:13.303
You bought IBM, and you could
express big numbers in a world

01:05:13.304 --> 01:05:17.050
without floating point, you
could represent accurately with.

01:05:17.051 --> 01:05:19.240
Okay, so there's some things
that really compelled people for

01:05:19.241 --> 01:05:20.670
business reasons to do that.

01:05:20.671 --> 01:05:23.510
But okay, PASCAL came along and
that was really

01:05:23.511 --> 01:05:25.540
successful because everybody
was forced to learn it.

01:05:25.541 --> 01:05:26.625
It wasn't successful, but for

01:05:26.626 --> 01:05:28.200
a while it looked like
it was successful.

01:05:28.201 --> 01:05:31.663
Everybody was forced to learn
in computer science departments

01:05:31.664 --> 01:05:33.830
which were all like
new in the 80s.

01:05:33.831 --> 01:05:36.627
Visual Basic came along, I
remember making fun of a friend

01:05:36.628 --> 01:05:39.367
of mine who was looking for
Visual Basic programmers cuz I

01:05:39.368 --> 01:05:41.510
was a C, C++,
that kind of thing.

01:05:41.511 --> 01:05:42.410
And he says, look.

01:05:42.411 --> 01:05:44.872
My experience, and again,
his experience is that,

01:05:44.873 --> 01:05:47.290
he was able to get cheaper and
more productive guys.

01:05:47.291 --> 01:05:48.695
He ran a consulting business,

01:05:48.696 --> 01:05:50.591
who could actually
produce code faster.

01:05:50.592 --> 01:05:52.628
And so they have an argument
that people got into that, and

01:05:52.629 --> 01:05:54.416
unless they're in it,
yes, they're used to it,

01:05:54.417 --> 01:05:55.600
they know the language.

01:05:55.601 --> 01:05:58.684
We tried to get them to go to
C#, no like, what're you doing,

01:05:58.685 --> 01:05:59.804
we love Visual Basic.

01:05:59.805 --> 01:06:02.863
And then the Web thing comes,
and they're all different

01:06:02.864 --> 01:06:06.369
reasons, maybe, why people found
something that became a great

01:06:06.370 --> 01:06:09.893
tool for what they had to do,
and then the thing becomes.

01:06:09.894 --> 01:06:12.354
But I think the reasons
actually are kind of dirty,

01:06:12.355 --> 01:06:15.230
like let's try and illustrate
here about why it happens.

01:06:15.231 --> 01:06:19.118
So this thing you were saying
about understanding kind of

01:06:19.119 --> 01:06:23.492
the human nature aspect of how
humans interact with programming

01:06:23.493 --> 01:06:27.627
languages and environments is
probably far more relevant.

01:06:27.628 --> 01:06:30.719
It sort of goes back in, saying,
all the analysis of types and

01:06:30.720 --> 01:06:33.455
what languages should look like,
productivity, and

01:06:33.456 --> 01:06:36.790
10% improvement here is a social
human factor, it seems to be.

01:06:36.791 --> 01:06:38.748
&gt;&gt; Well, I don't think
necessarily that Meyerovich

01:06:38.749 --> 01:06:39.801
would disagree with that.

01:06:39.802 --> 01:06:41.743
These human constructs,
that's why I'm saying,

01:06:41.744 --> 01:06:42.942
it's kind of a social problem.

01:06:42.943 --> 01:06:45.460
Right, this is what I mean
by that, is we're saying

01:06:45.461 --> 01:06:48.282
effectively the same thing
in very different ways, but-

01:06:48.283 --> 01:06:49.081
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean,

01:06:49.082 --> 01:06:51.546
it's [INAUDIBLE] you gave
about communities and

01:06:51.547 --> 01:06:52.620
things like that.

01:06:52.621 --> 01:06:54.260
I think for
someone which is, but

01:06:54.261 --> 01:06:56.760
I think there are a whole
bunch of different reasons.

01:06:56.761 --> 01:06:59.624
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I agree, and that's
actually what the [INAUDIBLE]

01:06:59.625 --> 01:07:02.199
paper shows,
is that it's complicated, right?

01:07:02.200 --> 01:07:04.013
Adoption's a complicated thing,
yeah.

01:07:04.014 --> 01:07:07.199
&gt;&gt; So I wanted to ask kind of
two questions that are related

01:07:07.200 --> 01:07:10.190
to each other, which is
about how do you evolve?

01:07:10.191 --> 01:07:14.970
So the first question is about,
let's say a language came out.

01:07:14.971 --> 01:07:17.540
There's like 15
studies that show that

01:07:17.541 --> 01:07:19.060
the use of angle brackets for

01:07:19.061 --> 01:07:23.180
generics turns out to be worse
than if you use something else.

01:07:23.181 --> 01:07:25.960
How do you evolve that language,
given that it's already out

01:07:25.961 --> 01:07:28.090
there, and there's code
written in that language?

01:07:28.091 --> 01:07:29.573
And the second thing is,

01:07:29.574 --> 01:07:32.681
we have a community of
researchers who apparently in

01:07:32.682 --> 01:07:36.932
software engineering don't often
do randomized controlled trials.

01:07:36.933 --> 01:07:40.313
How do you evolve them to start
doing that without saying,

01:07:40.314 --> 01:07:42.590
tomorrow the standard
is changed, and

01:07:42.591 --> 01:07:46.247
we're gonna reject all papers
that don't do these randomized

01:07:46.248 --> 01:07:47.910
control trials perfectly.

01:07:47.911 --> 01:07:50.568
Which is the kind of thing
that's actually happening with

01:07:50.569 --> 01:07:52.313
user studies in some
of these fields.

01:07:52.314 --> 01:07:54.714
Like in the ASC conference or
in FSC,

01:07:54.715 --> 01:07:57.508
where it's not uncommon
to user studies.

01:07:57.509 --> 01:07:59.905
Now all of a sudden people who
are good at evaluating user

01:07:59.906 --> 01:08:02.650
studies are like, well,
that's not done properly, so

01:08:02.651 --> 01:08:04.030
I'm gonna reject the paper.

01:08:04.031 --> 01:08:08.070
So there's no way to teach
the community how to do

01:08:08.071 --> 01:08:09.960
these things without
making them perfect.

01:08:09.961 --> 01:08:13.220
How do you evolve both
the languages, the programs, and

01:08:13.221 --> 01:08:17.240
the people over time to
get to better standards?

01:08:17.241 --> 01:08:20.402
&gt;&gt; Boy, both of those questions
are super hard, [LAUGH] so

01:08:20.403 --> 01:08:21.400
thanks.

01:08:21.401 --> 01:08:25.650
No, let me do the second one
first, because I think that

01:08:25.651 --> 01:08:27.870
it's not necessarily easier
from a social perspective.

01:08:27.871 --> 01:08:30.040
But I think that you're
completely right.

01:08:30.041 --> 01:08:32.980
The implication of
the question is that

01:08:32.981 --> 01:08:35.030
this is not boolean, right?

01:08:35.031 --> 01:08:36.712
Not only is it not the case
that all papers should be

01:08:36.713 --> 01:08:38.404
randomized controlled trials,
that's weird.

01:08:38.405 --> 01:08:41.823
That there needs to be
coherence, but in addition to

01:08:41.824 --> 01:08:45.660
that, even people's
understanding isn't boolean.

01:08:45.661 --> 01:08:48.200
We don't teach how to do
this stuff in school,

01:08:48.201 --> 01:08:50.530
not really,
at least in computer science.

01:08:50.531 --> 01:08:53.050
We don't really teach it at
conferences, and you can't fly

01:08:53.051 --> 01:08:55.460
around to every conference and
do workshops and stuff.

01:08:55.461 --> 01:08:58.710
I know there's been some, and
I've done some, but that's hard,

01:08:58.711 --> 01:09:00.750
so the question is,
how do you do it?

01:09:00.751 --> 01:09:03.050
And the answer is,
I don't know, exactly,

01:09:03.051 --> 01:09:06.170
I think this is a problem
that's bigger than me, right?

01:09:06.171 --> 01:09:07.440
But my suspicion is,

01:09:07.441 --> 01:09:08.850
there's probably
an educational component,

01:09:08.851 --> 01:09:12.060
we should try to get it in
schools more over time.

01:09:12.061 --> 01:09:17.150
I think that we should try
to get it into conferences,

01:09:17.151 --> 01:09:19.360
in terms of workshops
to help people.

01:09:19.361 --> 01:09:21.210
I think that would be good.

01:09:21.211 --> 01:09:24.690
I think that The National
Science Foundation giving money

01:09:24.691 --> 01:09:28.640
to it, meaning they should
get communities together that

01:09:28.641 --> 01:09:30.660
are both scientists and
practitioners.

01:09:30.661 --> 01:09:34.530
We need people that are in the
trenches not only to study but

01:09:34.531 --> 01:09:35.360
to give feedback.

01:09:35.361 --> 01:09:38.750
But we also need people that
understand all the data and

01:09:38.751 --> 01:09:39.820
stuff like that.

01:09:39.821 --> 01:09:42.480
In medicine, they took
a totally different tact for

01:09:42.481 --> 01:09:45.700
changing it, and that was
they changed the law, right?

01:09:45.701 --> 01:09:49.600
And specifically,
what happened was, in the 40s,

01:09:49.601 --> 01:09:53.100
doctors didn't wanna use
the scientific method, right?

01:09:53.101 --> 01:09:54.362
Cuz they believed
they were right, and

01:09:54.363 --> 01:09:55.776
they were trying to
help their patients, so

01:09:55.777 --> 01:09:56.665
Congress changed the law.

01:09:56.666 --> 01:09:59.434
And they started to say, this
happened really early, actually.

01:09:59.435 --> 01:10:02.868
So in 1902, they changed the law
so that if you were to give

01:10:02.869 --> 01:10:05.395
a drug, you had to say
what was in it, right?

01:10:05.396 --> 01:10:10.530
And that sounds today totally
obvious, but you had to say what

01:10:10.531 --> 01:10:15.880
was in it, but you didn't have
to say whether it works, right?

01:10:15.881 --> 01:10:18.853
And then in 1927, they created
the FDA, and they said, okay,

01:10:18.854 --> 01:10:21.581
well, we don't know what the
answer is or how to evaluate it.

01:10:21.582 --> 01:10:25.349
But we do know that we should
check if it kills people, and

01:10:25.350 --> 01:10:26.613
sometimes it was.

01:10:26.614 --> 01:10:29.401
So the question is
then how do we proceed?

01:10:29.402 --> 01:10:33.670
Do we get communities to
have minimal policing and

01:10:33.671 --> 01:10:35.410
pop it up over time?

01:10:35.411 --> 01:10:38.200
Do we do training,
do we do all of the above?

01:10:38.201 --> 01:10:39.910
Since I'm kind of
a coherence guy,

01:10:39.911 --> 01:10:42.470
I kind of take all of
the above approach.

01:10:42.471 --> 01:10:44.900
But I really don't like
exactly what you just said,

01:10:44.901 --> 01:10:47.100
which is just like this
is totally imperfect,

01:10:47.101 --> 01:10:48.380
we need to reject
everything like that.

01:10:48.381 --> 01:10:49.170
I think that's nuts,

01:10:49.171 --> 01:10:52.016
I think this is learning, right,
researchers learn too, right?

01:10:52.017 --> 01:10:54.307
Maybe you disagree,
but I doubt you do.

01:10:54.308 --> 01:10:57.327
But yeah, okay, so
then there's the first question,

01:10:57.328 --> 01:11:00.230
which is, what do we do
about evolving the language?

01:11:00.231 --> 01:11:03.400
I think this is actually
really hard, so

01:11:03.401 --> 01:11:07.870
I like to solve really easy
problems, right, or try to.

01:11:07.871 --> 01:11:10.507
So there's been a number of
approaches I think we all know.

01:11:10.508 --> 01:11:12.798
Some languages take
the approach of,

01:11:12.799 --> 01:11:15.376
we're only going to
add the language, and

01:11:15.377 --> 01:11:18.180
we never change anything
that came before it.

01:11:18.181 --> 01:11:20.903
And then there's the opposite,
like the Python 3 to 2 debate,

01:11:20.904 --> 01:11:23.340
how many people know that,
I don't.

01:11:23.341 --> 01:11:25.040
So it's kinda like
they changed some stuff

01:11:25.041 --> 01:11:27.022
that broke backward
compatibility.

01:11:27.023 --> 01:11:30.170
And so I'll tell you two things
that we do in our lab, but

01:11:30.171 --> 01:11:34.190
we have it easy
compared to all of you.

01:11:34.191 --> 01:11:34.890
In my lab,

01:11:34.891 --> 01:11:37.060
we developed a programming
language called Quorum.

01:11:37.061 --> 01:11:39.160
It's only used by
about 2500 kids, but

01:11:39.161 --> 01:11:41.728
that's still a lot of kids,
right, over the world.

01:11:41.729 --> 01:11:43.318
And so we make a rule, we say,

01:11:43.319 --> 01:11:46.393
we're gonna break backward
compatibility once a year.

01:11:46.394 --> 01:11:49.365
And we can do that because it's
mostly teachers when you work in

01:11:49.366 --> 01:11:50.690
education.

01:11:50.691 --> 01:11:53.715
The next generation of kids that
comes around the next year does

01:11:53.716 --> 01:11:56.468
not care at all, right,
because that's not the point.

01:11:56.469 --> 01:11:59.480
But that's not fair,
that's not an easy thing.

01:11:59.481 --> 01:12:03.970
If you're working in C++,
then you can't just say,

01:12:03.971 --> 01:12:06.530
sorry, we don't have
pointers anymore cuz

01:12:06.531 --> 01:12:08.450
you're gonna break
a little code, right?

01:12:08.451 --> 01:12:10.470
I'm gonna go on a limb, right?

01:12:10.471 --> 01:12:11.900
So the question is,
how do we do that?

01:12:11.901 --> 01:12:14.730
I mean, I think, again, it's a
multi-pronged thing, on the one

01:12:14.731 --> 01:12:18.000
hand, we have to get people to
understand even what evidence

01:12:18.001 --> 01:12:20.640
is, so that they think that
maybe there's a need for that.

01:12:20.641 --> 01:12:22.800
We need to train people
on what the impacts are,

01:12:22.801 --> 01:12:24.460
and go for the biggest ones.

01:12:24.461 --> 01:12:27.440
But we also have to do it
in such a way that we don't

01:12:27.441 --> 01:12:29.950
break the things that'll cause
the most harm to the community,

01:12:29.951 --> 01:12:32.580
like turning off pointers all of
a sudden, and stuff like that.

01:12:32.581 --> 01:12:34.520
Well, that's just an example,
but you get the idea,

01:12:34.521 --> 01:12:37.380
right, features that
are fundamentally game breaking.

01:12:37.381 --> 01:12:39.450
On the other hand,
it could be that for

01:12:39.451 --> 01:12:43.788
some of these languages, over a
20, 30, 50, or 100-year period,

01:12:43.789 --> 01:12:46.312
that we can start to change
a few things here and

01:12:46.313 --> 01:12:48.098
few things there on a schedule.

01:12:48.099 --> 01:12:50.739
Where everybody knows it's
coming, everybody knows what

01:12:50.740 --> 01:12:53.376
the plan is, and everybody
knows what the evidence is, and

01:12:53.377 --> 01:12:54.920
can try to refute it.

01:12:54.921 --> 01:12:58.388
Right, they can say, C++ on
this date is changing to this,

01:12:58.389 --> 01:13:00.671
and all of the compilers
that people do.

01:13:00.672 --> 01:13:03.172
And if you don't like that,
here's the study methodologies,

01:13:03.173 --> 01:13:04.929
the data for them, and
if you don't like it,

01:13:04.930 --> 01:13:05.918
you can try to refute it.

01:13:05.919 --> 01:13:11.075
But this is Andy's pipe
dream world where that-

01:13:11.076 --> 01:13:11.607
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:11.608 --> 01:13:12.644
&gt;&gt; Really, did they try that?

01:13:12.645 --> 01:13:15.783
&gt;&gt; I don't know about that,
but that's a great thing

01:13:15.784 --> 01:13:19.734
because earlier you said Y2K
only comes around however often.

01:13:19.735 --> 01:13:23.800
And you just came up with a way
of having it happen every few

01:13:23.801 --> 01:13:24.824
years [LAUGH].

01:13:24.825 --> 01:13:29.650
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well, I said-
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

01:13:29.651 --> 01:13:31.527
&gt;&gt; I don't know if that's quite

01:13:31.528 --> 01:13:35.063
fair because Y2K was a problem
that was deeply rooted.

01:13:35.064 --> 01:13:38.011
But like suppose we said,
it depends on the amount of

01:13:38.012 --> 01:13:40.635
change that you're requesting,
all right?

01:13:40.636 --> 01:13:46.105
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] old code perhaps-
&gt;&gt; Well, not any subtraction or

01:13:46.106 --> 01:13:50.660
change, so an addition wouldn't-
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's fair,

01:13:50.661 --> 01:13:53.421
if you had some rules about
that, but that [INAUDIBLE].

01:13:53.422 --> 01:13:55.639
&gt;&gt; So subtractions would need
more evidence than addition.

01:13:55.640 --> 01:13:57.406
You'd have to be sensible,
right?

01:13:57.407 --> 01:14:03.817
&gt;&gt; Does anyone know how many
version of C++ implementations

01:14:03.818 --> 01:14:08.826
there are that do not
parse by every compiler?

01:14:08.827 --> 01:14:09.368
&gt;&gt; Hundreds?

01:14:09.369 --> 01:14:10.799
&gt;&gt; There are literally hundreds,

01:14:10.800 --> 01:14:13.668
and that didn't involve that
many official versions of C++.

01:14:13.669 --> 01:14:17.160
And yet there are still so many
that can't read certain, lots of

01:14:17.161 --> 01:14:20.661
code by Microsoft can't be read
by non-Microsoft compilers.

01:14:20.662 --> 01:14:21.842
&gt;&gt; Yes, that's true, but

01:14:21.843 --> 01:14:24.543
I discovered an interest in
[INAUDIBLE] Visual Studio team

01:14:24.544 --> 01:14:25.170
[INAUDIBLE].

01:14:25.171 --> 01:14:26.736
And it was this that,

01:14:26.737 --> 01:14:31.086
there was something about who's
checking into our tree and

01:14:31.087 --> 01:14:35.275
doing some stuff and signing
bugs and people [INAUDIBLE].

01:14:35.276 --> 01:14:38.810
I discovered that, [INAUDIBLE]
color changed everything.

01:14:38.811 --> 01:14:42.010
That there's this one guy who's
checking in all of these changes

01:14:42.011 --> 01:14:43.990
to virtually everything
throughout the entire tree.

01:14:43.991 --> 01:14:47.390
It turned out to be the guy from
Visual Studio whose job it was

01:14:47.391 --> 01:14:50.797
to make our code work with the
latest compilers [INAUDIBLE].

01:14:50.798 --> 01:14:53.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Nice, yeah?

01:14:53.631 --> 01:14:57.459
&gt;&gt; So it's very agreeable, and
kind of obvious the way you've

01:14:57.460 --> 01:15:01.062
laid it out, that we should
have more control studies and

01:15:01.063 --> 01:15:03.331
better examples for
these things.

01:15:03.332 --> 01:15:04.301
But I wanted to ask,

01:15:04.302 --> 01:15:07.097
the example in almost all of
your The running theme in all of

01:15:07.098 --> 01:15:09.278
your examples is this
idea of productivity.

01:15:09.279 --> 01:15:11.324
And I'm wondering if that's,

01:15:11.325 --> 01:15:13.950
is that an innocent
choice of metric?

01:15:13.951 --> 01:15:16.522
Why is that the thing that
we're looking to optimize for

01:15:16.523 --> 01:15:17.650
our study?

01:15:17.651 --> 01:15:19.013
&gt;&gt; No, that's a great question,
actually.

01:15:19.014 --> 01:15:21.617
So it's not the only thing
that has been studied in

01:15:21.618 --> 01:15:24.905
the literature, but it's one
that is often mentioned in terms

01:15:24.906 --> 01:15:26.980
of time, because time costs.

01:15:26.981 --> 01:15:28.799
That's usually why
people study it.

01:15:28.800 --> 01:15:33.163
For example, if you know that
you're gonna reduce the time

01:15:33.164 --> 01:15:35.239
to fix compiler error.

01:15:35.240 --> 01:15:36.696
Here's an example.

01:15:36.697 --> 01:15:40.042
There's a natural study,
not a controlled study, but

01:15:40.043 --> 01:15:43.471
it's a really excellent
natural study by Brett Becker.

01:15:43.472 --> 01:15:46.072
This guy in the UK that is kind
of a young dude that maybe isn't

01:15:46.073 --> 01:15:47.653
that well known yet,
but should be,

01:15:47.654 --> 01:15:49.860
because his work is fantastic.

01:15:49.861 --> 01:15:54.236
And Brett did this study on
changing compiler errors to be

01:15:54.237 --> 01:15:56.251
more friendly to people.

01:15:56.252 --> 01:15:59.491
And he did just really obvious
things, like instead of saying,

01:15:59.492 --> 01:16:02.800
eat my face, it would say, hey,
there's an error on line five.

01:16:03.840 --> 01:16:06.323
It's a terrible example, I
don't know why I said that, but

01:16:06.324 --> 01:16:07.112
just bear with it.

01:16:07.113 --> 01:16:08.164
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The point is,

01:16:08.165 --> 01:16:11.315
he would take messages that
were perhaps esoteric, and

01:16:11.316 --> 01:16:13.000
he would do his best.

01:16:13.001 --> 01:16:16.250
He didn't have a methodology for
knowing what to change in them,

01:16:16.251 --> 01:16:17.323
but he wanted to try.

01:16:17.324 --> 01:16:21.146
And he found some percentage
decrease in the amount of time

01:16:21.147 --> 01:16:22.700
to remove errors.

01:16:22.701 --> 01:16:25.610
Now in that case, it's time, so
you could call it productivity.

01:16:25.611 --> 01:16:28.182
But he was thinking that
if that was a developer,

01:16:28.183 --> 01:16:29.572
that would cost, right?

01:16:29.573 --> 01:16:30.910
So that costs money.

01:16:30.911 --> 01:16:31.870
But on the other hand,

01:16:31.871 --> 01:16:34.950
that's obviously not the only
thing that you care about.

01:16:34.951 --> 01:16:36.727
You could analyze
comprehension metrics,

01:16:36.728 --> 01:16:37.934
you could analyze accuracy.

01:16:37.935 --> 01:16:40.398
So sometimes we do accuracy
studies for syntax,

01:16:40.399 --> 01:16:43.710
like how accurately can they put
in the syntax, stuff like that.

01:16:43.711 --> 01:16:45.930
And I think all of those
things should be studied.

01:16:45.931 --> 01:16:47.313
Again, it's coherence, right?

01:16:47.314 --> 01:16:51.248
You want all of these studies to
know the totality of evidence.

01:16:51.249 --> 01:16:54.191
But time is a common theme for
that reason, so yeah?

01:16:54.192 --> 01:16:57.184
&gt;&gt; Well, there's a dominant
thing that's changing,

01:16:57.185 --> 01:16:58.248
it is in software,

01:16:58.249 --> 01:17:01.453
which is not the time you
spend developing the software.

01:17:01.454 --> 01:17:03.123
It's always the maintenance and

01:17:03.124 --> 01:17:06.288
things like that that seem to be
a bigger cost factor probably.

01:17:06.289 --> 01:17:07.818
&gt;&gt; Right,
saw that notes of that.

01:17:07.819 --> 01:17:10.528
&gt;&gt; But
now it's the vulnerabilities.

01:17:10.529 --> 01:17:11.947
&gt;&gt; The what?

01:17:11.948 --> 01:17:13.860
&gt;&gt; The vulnerabilities
in software.

01:17:13.861 --> 01:17:16.703
By producing software with
vulnerabilities, every day you

01:17:16.704 --> 01:17:19.322
read in the paper that the
tremendous cost to society and

01:17:19.323 --> 01:17:22.082
companies and everything
else by the vulnerabilities.

01:17:22.083 --> 01:17:22.974
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great idea.

01:17:22.975 --> 01:17:24.424
I've heard that before, right?

01:17:24.425 --> 01:17:26.928
I gave a talk at CME once and
someone mentioned that.

01:17:26.929 --> 01:17:29.171
And I think it's spot on,
right, why not?

01:17:29.172 --> 01:17:32.836
Why not try to figure out can
we reduce that a little bit too?

01:17:32.837 --> 01:17:34.513
That's great, yeah.

01:17:34.514 --> 01:17:37.209
Now this is what I love about
this coherence idea is it

01:17:37.210 --> 01:17:40.460
doesn't say, this person's
right, that person's wrong,

01:17:40.461 --> 01:17:41.510
whatever.

01:17:41.511 --> 01:17:43.458
It says, why don't we
just look at it all and

01:17:43.459 --> 01:17:44.940
then try to reverse engineer it.

01:17:44.941 --> 01:17:46.169
Natural studies are good,

01:17:46.170 --> 01:17:48.360
controlled studies have
a different purpose and

01:17:48.361 --> 01:17:50.960
they work at different scales
for a good reason, right?

01:17:50.961 --> 01:17:52.880
They let you try crazy things,
right?

01:17:52.881 --> 01:17:57.492
Anyway, other questions?

01:17:57.493 --> 01:17:58.911
All right, well,
thank you very much for coming,

01:17:58.912 --> 01:17:59.415
I appreciate it.

01:17:59.416 --> 01:18:02.897
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

